window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-12-2", "source": "2021年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university student union is planning to hold a speech contest. You are now to write a proposal for organizing the contest. The proposal may include the topic, aim , procedure and selection of contestants. You will have 30 minutes to write the proposal. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "A Proposal for a Speech Contest On behalf of the Student Union, I am writing to propose that our university\nhold a campus speech contest next month, and the details are outlined below for your approval.\nThe contest will focus on the theme \"My Chinese Dream.\" Our aim is to sharpen students' public-speaking\nskills, build their confidence, and enrich campus cultural life.\nAs for the procedure, we will first post a notice to call for entries. Then a preliminary round will be held\nin each college. Finally, twelve finalists will deliver their speeches on stage, scored by a panel of teachers\nand students.\nThe selection of contestants will be fair and open: applicants must submit a written draft, and those with clear\nideas and fluent expression will be shortlisted through a short interview. We sincerely hope you will approve\nthis proposal, and any suggestions would be warmly welcomed. The Student Union"}, "translation": {"prompt": "坎儿井(Karez)是新疆干旱地区的一种水利系统，由地下渠道将水井连接而成。该系统将春夏季节渗入 (seep into) 地下的大量雨水及积雪融水收集起来，通过山体的自然坡度引到地面，用于灌溉农田和满足人们的日常用水需求。坎儿井减少了水在地面的蒸发 (evaporation)， 对地表破坏很小，因而有效地保护了自然资源与生态环境。坎儿井体现了我国人民与自然和谐共存的智慧，是对人类文明的一大贡献。", "sample": "Karez is a water conservancy system in the arid areas of Xinjiang, formed by underground channels that connect a series of wells. The system collects large amounts of rainwater and meltwater that seep into the ground in spring and summer, and channels them to the surface through the natural slope of the mountains to irrigate farmland and meet people's daily water needs. Karez reduces the evaporation of water from the ground and causes little damage to the surface, and thus effectively protects natural resources and the ecological environment. It embodies the wisdom of the Chinese people in living in harmony with nature, and is a great contribution to human civilization."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2021-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "It is commonly believed that the great English dramatist and poet William Shakespeare was born in Stratford-on-Avon on April 23, 1564. But it is impossible to know the  __26__  day on which he was born.\nChurch records show he was baptized(施洗礼) on April 26, and three days was a customary amount of time to wait before baptizing a newly born baby. Shakespeare's date of death is  __27__  known, however: it was April 23, 1616. He was 52 and had retired to Stratford three years before.\nAlthough few plays have been performed or analyzed as extensively as the 38 plays Shakespeare wrote, there are few surviving details about his life. This  __28__  of biographical information is due primarily to his social  __29__  ; he was not a noble, but the son of a leather trader.\nShakespeare  __30__  attended the grammar school in Stratford, where he would have studied Latin and read  __31__  literature. He did not go to university and at age 18 married Anne Hathaway, who was eight years his  __32__  . They had four children, including the twins, Hamnet and Judith. Nothing is known of the period between the birth of the twins and Shakespeare's  __33__  as a dramatist in London in the early 1590s.\nIn a million words written over 20 years, he  __34__  the full range of human emotions and conflicts with a  __35__  that remains sharp today. As his great contemporary the poet and dramatist Ben Jonson said, \"He was not of an age, but for all time.\"", "matchIntro": "How to not be boring", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Humans are creatures of habit. We love to establish a routine and stick with it. Then we often put ourselves on auto-pilot. Routines can be incredibly useful in helping you get things done. However, too much of a routine can also make you incredibly boring. Nevertheless, many people live lives that are boringly predictable, or live a life where everything is outlined or planned."}, {"label": "B", "text": "To tell the truth, interesting people are more popular among their friends. If you don't arouse someone's curiosity or brighten someone's day, you probably come across as being a little bit dull. But that doesn't mean your life has ended and you can't do anything to change it. If you find yourself searching for something to say beyond small talk, try these tactics to find more interesting approaches to conversation."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Recently, I was at a gathering of colleagues when someone turned to me and asked, \"So, what's new with you?\" Ordinarily, I think I'm a good conversationalist. After all, it's literally my job to talk to people and tell their stories or share their advice. And that's not exactly an unexpected question. Still, the only \"new-to-me\" topics that came to mind were my daughter's basketball tournament(锦标赛) and my feelings about that morning's political headlines—neither amusing nor appropriate topics at that moment."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oh, no, I thought. Have I become boring? But sharing our experiences in an authentic way to connect with other people is what makes us interesting, says associate professor Michael Pirson. The hesitation I felt in not sharing the ordinary things that were happening in my life, and the wild mental search for something more interesting, may have backfired and made me seem less interesting."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"If someone is making up some conversation that might be interesting, it's probably not going to land well,\" says Pirson, whose expertise includes trust and well-being, mindfulness, and humanistic management. \"It's going to feel like a made-up conversation that people don't necessarily want to tune in to.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "The most interesting people aren't those who've gone on some Eat, Pray, Love journey to find themselves. Instead, Pirson says, they're those who examine the ordinary. \"Often, the 'boring things' may not be boring at all. Maybe they are actually little miracles,\" he says. Share your observations about the world around you—interesting stories you heard or things you noticed—and you may be surprised by the universal connection they inspire."}, {"label": "G", "text": "This is essentially how Jessica Hagy starts her day. The author of How to Be Interesting: An Instruction Manual, Hagy spends a lot of time thinking about what's interesting to her. People who are interesting are persistently curious, she says."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Think about the everyday things around you and ask questions about them. What is that roadside monument I see on my way to work every day? Who built that interesting building in my city? What nearby attractions haven't I visited? Why do people do things that way? Use what you find to ask more questions and learn more about the world around you. \"Having that sort of curiosity is almost like a protective gear from getting into boredom,\" she says. And when you find things that are truly interesting to you, share them."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Television veteran Audrey Morrissey, executive producer of NBC's The Voice, is always looking for what will make a person or story interesting to viewers: It's usually a matter of individuality. \"Having a strong point of view, signature style, or being a super-enthusiast in a particular field makes someone interesting,\" she says. That means embracing what is truly interesting or unique about yourself. \"Many people are 'not boring' in the way that they can carry a conversation or can be good at a social gathering, etc. To be interesting means that you have lived life, taken risks, traveled, sought out experience to learn for yourself and share with others,\" she says."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Of course, it's possible to be a fountain of knowledge and a boring person, says public relations consultant Andrea Pass. Paying attention to the listener is an important part of having a conversation that's interesting to both parties. Talking on and on about what's interesting to you isn't going to make you an interesting person, she says."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"If the listener is not paying attention, it's your sign to shorten the story or change direction. Make sure to bring the audience into the conversation so that it is not one-sided,\" Pass says. Be a better listener yourself, and give others opportunities to participate in the conversation by inviting them with questions or requests to share their own experiences or thoughts. (e.g., \"Now, tell me about your favorite book,\" or \"Have you ever been to that attraction?\") Questions are a powerful tool, especially when they encourage others to disclose information about themselves. A 2012 study from the University of California, Santa Barbara, found that roughly 40% of the time we are talking, we're disclosing subjective information about our experience. And when we're doing so, our brains are more engaged. So one strategy to leave others with the impression that you're a sparkling conversation partner is to get others to talk about themselves."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Being relatable is also essential, Morrissey says. \"The best entertainment and storytelling comes from people who are relatable-those who don't shy away from opening up but freely share who they are and what they care about. These are the people viewers most relate to and find interesting. Being authentic, honest, and vulnerable is always interesting.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "I have now come to realize that being boring, in actuality, is not only about who you are as a person, but also how you present yourself. No matter what, make sure you are having fun in life. Because when you are enjoying, people around you will begin to enjoy as well. Show some interest in them and they will definitely show some in you. If you are a very reserved person, this could be a little difficult at first. But with a little effort, you can definitely improve."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "With obesity now affecting 29% of the population in England, and expected to rise to 35% by 2030, should we now recognise it as a disease? Obesity, in which excess body fat has accumulated to such an extent that health may be adversely affected, meets the dictionary definition of disease, argues Professor John Wilding. He points out that more than 200 genes influence weight. \"Thus body weight is strongly influenced by biology—it is not an individual's fault if they develop obesity.\" Yet the widespread view is that obesity is self-induced and that it is entirely the individual's responsibility to do something about it. Recognising obesity as a chronic disease with severe complications rather than a lifestyle choice \"should help reduce the stigma(耻辱) and discrimination experienced by many people with obesity,\" he adds.\nProfessor Wilding disagrees that labelling a high proportion of the population as having a disease removes personal responsibility or may overwhelm health services, pointing out that other common diseases, such as high blood pressure and diabetes, require people to take action to manage their condition. He suggests that most people with obesity will eventually develop complications. \"But unless we accept that obesity is a disease, we are not going to be able to tackle it,\" he concludes.\nBut Dr. Richard Pile, a physician with a special interest in diabetes, argues that adopting this approach \"could actually result in worse outcomes for individuals and society.\" He believes that the dictionary definition of disease \"is so vague that we can classify almost anything as a disease\" and says the question is not whether we can, but whether we should, and to what end.\nIf labelling obesity as a disease was harmless then it wouldn't really matter, he writes. But labelling obesity as a disease \"risks reducing autonomy, disempowering and robbing people of the intrinsic(内在的) motivation that is such an important enabler of change.\" What's more, making obesity a disease \"may not benefit patients, but it will benefit healthcare providers and the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry when health insurance and clinical guidelines promote treatment with drugs and surgery,\" he warns.", "47": "With obesity now affecting 29% of the population in England, and expected to rise to 35% by 2030, should we now recognise it as a disease? Obesity, in which excess body fat has accumulated to such an extent that health may be adversely affected, meets the dictionary definition of disease, argues Professor John Wilding. He points out that more than 200 genes influence weight. \"Thus body weight is strongly influenced by biology—it is not an individual's fault if they develop obesity.\" Yet the widespread view is that obesity is self-induced and that it is entirely the individual's responsibility to do something about it. Recognising obesity as a chronic disease with severe complications rather than a lifestyle choice \"should help reduce the stigma(耻辱) and discrimination experienced by many people with obesity,\" he adds.\nProfessor Wilding disagrees that labelling a high proportion of the population as having a disease removes personal responsibility or may overwhelm health services, pointing out that other common diseases, such as high blood pressure and diabetes, require people to take action to manage their condition. He suggests that most people with obesity will eventually develop complications. \"But unless we accept that obesity is a disease, we are not going to be able to tackle it,\" he concludes.\nBut Dr. Richard Pile, a physician with a special interest in diabetes, argues that adopting this approach \"could actually result in worse outcomes for individuals and society.\" He believes that the dictionary definition of disease \"is so vague that we can classify almost anything as a disease\" and says the question is not whether we can, but whether we should, and to what end.\nIf labelling obesity as a disease was harmless then it wouldn't really matter, he writes. But labelling obesity as a disease \"risks reducing autonomy, disempowering and robbing people of the intrinsic(内在的) motivation that is such an important enabler of change.\" What's more, making obesity a disease \"may not benefit patients, but it will benefit healthcare providers and the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry when health insurance and clinical guidelines promote treatment with drugs and surgery,\" he warns.", "48": "With obesity now affecting 29% of the population in England, and expected to rise to 35% by 2030, should we now recognise it as a disease? Obesity, in which excess body fat has accumulated to such an extent that health may be adversely affected, meets the dictionary definition of disease, argues Professor John Wilding. He points out that more than 200 genes influence weight. \"Thus body weight is strongly influenced by biology—it is not an individual's fault if they develop obesity.\" Yet the widespread view is that obesity is self-induced and that it is entirely the individual's responsibility to do something about it. Recognising obesity as a chronic disease with severe complications rather than a lifestyle choice \"should help reduce the stigma(耻辱) and discrimination experienced by many people with obesity,\" he adds.\nProfessor Wilding disagrees that labelling a high proportion of the population as having a disease removes personal responsibility or may overwhelm health services, pointing out that other common diseases, such as high blood pressure and diabetes, require people to take action to manage their condition. He suggests that most people with obesity will eventually develop complications. \"But unless we accept that obesity is a disease, we are not going to be able to tackle it,\" he concludes.\nBut Dr. Richard Pile, a physician with a special interest in diabetes, argues that adopting this approach \"could actually result in worse outcomes for individuals and society.\" He believes that the dictionary definition of disease \"is so vague that we can classify almost anything as a disease\" and says the question is not whether we can, but whether we should, and to what end.\nIf labelling obesity as a disease was harmless then it wouldn't really matter, he writes. But labelling obesity as a disease \"risks reducing autonomy, disempowering and robbing people of the intrinsic(内在的) motivation that is such an important enabler of change.\" What's more, making obesity a disease \"may not benefit patients, but it will benefit healthcare providers and the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry when health insurance and clinical guidelines promote treatment with drugs and surgery,\" he warns.", "49": "With obesity now affecting 29% of the population in England, and expected to rise to 35% by 2030, should we now recognise it as a disease? Obesity, in which excess body fat has accumulated to such an extent that health may be adversely affected, meets the dictionary definition of disease, argues Professor John Wilding. He points out that more than 200 genes influence weight. \"Thus body weight is strongly influenced by biology—it is not an individual's fault if they develop obesity.\" Yet the widespread view is that obesity is self-induced and that it is entirely the individual's responsibility to do something about it. Recognising obesity as a chronic disease with severe complications rather than a lifestyle choice \"should help reduce the stigma(耻辱) and discrimination experienced by many people with obesity,\" he adds.\nProfessor Wilding disagrees that labelling a high proportion of the population as having a disease removes personal responsibility or may overwhelm health services, pointing out that other common diseases, such as high blood pressure and diabetes, require people to take action to manage their condition. He suggests that most people with obesity will eventually develop complications. \"But unless we accept that obesity is a disease, we are not going to be able to tackle it,\" he concludes.\nBut Dr. Richard Pile, a physician with a special interest in diabetes, argues that adopting this approach \"could actually result in worse outcomes for individuals and society.\" He believes that the dictionary definition of disease \"is so vague that we can classify almost anything as a disease\" and says the question is not whether we can, but whether we should, and to what end.\nIf labelling obesity as a disease was harmless then it wouldn't really matter, he writes. But labelling obesity as a disease \"risks reducing autonomy, disempowering and robbing people of the intrinsic(内在的) motivation that is such an important enabler of change.\" What's more, making obesity a disease \"may not benefit patients, but it will benefit healthcare providers and the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry when health insurance and clinical guidelines promote treatment with drugs and surgery,\" he warns.", "50": "With obesity now affecting 29% of the population in England, and expected to rise to 35% by 2030, should we now recognise it as a disease? Obesity, in which excess body fat has accumulated to such an extent that health may be adversely affected, meets the dictionary definition of disease, argues Professor John Wilding. He points out that more than 200 genes influence weight. \"Thus body weight is strongly influenced by biology—it is not an individual's fault if they develop obesity.\" Yet the widespread view is that obesity is self-induced and that it is entirely the individual's responsibility to do something about it. Recognising obesity as a chronic disease with severe complications rather than a lifestyle choice \"should help reduce the stigma(耻辱) and discrimination experienced by many people with obesity,\" he adds.\nProfessor Wilding disagrees that labelling a high proportion of the population as having a disease removes personal responsibility or may overwhelm health services, pointing out that other common diseases, such as high blood pressure and diabetes, require people to take action to manage their condition. He suggests that most people with obesity will eventually develop complications. \"But unless we accept that obesity is a disease, we are not going to be able to tackle it,\" he concludes.\nBut Dr. Richard Pile, a physician with a special interest in diabetes, argues that adopting this approach \"could actually result in worse outcomes for individuals and society.\" He believes that the dictionary definition of disease \"is so vague that we can classify almost anything as a disease\" and says the question is not whether we can, but whether we should, and to what end.\nIf labelling obesity as a disease was harmless then it wouldn't really matter, he writes. But labelling obesity as a disease \"risks reducing autonomy, disempowering and robbing people of the intrinsic(内在的) motivation that is such an important enabler of change.\" What's more, making obesity a disease \"may not benefit patients, but it will benefit healthcare providers and the pharmaceutical(制药的) industry when health insurance and clinical guidelines promote treatment with drugs and surgery,\" he warns.", "51": "Nationwide, only about three percent of early childhood teachers are male in the U.S. Experts say this can have an impact on young children whose understanding of gender roles and identity are rapidly forming. Research has found that having access to diverse teachers is beneficial for children. For the youngest learners, it means they are more likely to get exposed to different varieties of play and communication. It also helps them develop healthy ideas around gender.\n\"In our world and our society, we have very specific stereotypes(模式化形象) of gender roles,\" said Mindi Reich-Shapiro, an assistant professor in the teacher education department of the Borough of Manhattan Community College, and one of the authors of a recent study. \"It's important for children to see other possibilities and other paths they can take.\"\nDespite mostly feeling supported by colleagues and family members, many of the male educators surveyed in the study reported facing social or cultural resistance in their careers as early education teachers. Some also reported that there were parents surprised or concerned that their child had a male teacher. And they had been advised by colleagues or other staff not to hug children.\nReich-Shapiro and fellow researchers made several recommendations to increase male representation in the field. Low pay has long been acknowledged as a major issue in the early childhood field. Over 70% of male educators who said they intended to stay in the early education workforce noted an increased salary was a major motivating factor for them to commit to the career long-term. The report suggests paying all early childhood educators the way elementary school teachers are paid.\nCities and programs should establish support groups for male early childhood educators and provide mentoring and professional development advice for male educators and their program leaders.\nThe authors also suggest that traditional recruitment approaches for early childhood educators \"do not address the gender gap in the field.\" They recommend providing young men opportunities to work with children through training and volunteer programs, targeting groups of men who are considering a career change, such as fathers.", "52": "Nationwide, only about three percent of early childhood teachers are male in the U.S. Experts say this can have an impact on young children whose understanding of gender roles and identity are rapidly forming. Research has found that having access to diverse teachers is beneficial for children. For the youngest learners, it means they are more likely to get exposed to different varieties of play and communication. It also helps them develop healthy ideas around gender.\n\"In our world and our society, we have very specific stereotypes(模式化形象) of gender roles,\" said Mindi Reich-Shapiro, an assistant professor in the teacher education department of the Borough of Manhattan Community College, and one of the authors of a recent study. \"It's important for children to see other possibilities and other paths they can take.\"\nDespite mostly feeling supported by colleagues and family members, many of the male educators surveyed in the study reported facing social or cultural resistance in their careers as early education teachers. Some also reported that there were parents surprised or concerned that their child had a male teacher. And they had been advised by colleagues or other staff not to hug children.\nReich-Shapiro and fellow researchers made several recommendations to increase male representation in the field. Low pay has long been acknowledged as a major issue in the early childhood field. Over 70% of male educators who said they intended to stay in the early education workforce noted an increased salary was a major motivating factor for them to commit to the career long-term. The report suggests paying all early childhood educators the way elementary school teachers are paid.\nCities and programs should establish support groups for male early childhood educators and provide mentoring and professional development advice for male educators and their program leaders.\nThe authors also suggest that traditional recruitment approaches for early childhood educators \"do not address the gender gap in the field.\" They recommend providing young men opportunities to work with children through training and volunteer programs, targeting groups of men who are considering a career change, such as fathers.", "53": "Nationwide, only about three percent of early childhood teachers are male in the U.S. Experts say this can have an impact on young children whose understanding of gender roles and identity are rapidly forming. Research has found that having access to diverse teachers is beneficial for children. For the youngest learners, it means they are more likely to get exposed to different varieties of play and communication. It also helps them develop healthy ideas around gender.\n\"In our world and our society, we have very specific stereotypes(模式化形象) of gender roles,\" said Mindi Reich-Shapiro, an assistant professor in the teacher education department of the Borough of Manhattan Community College, and one of the authors of a recent study. \"It's important for children to see other possibilities and other paths they can take.\"\nDespite mostly feeling supported by colleagues and family members, many of the male educators surveyed in the study reported facing social or cultural resistance in their careers as early education teachers. Some also reported that there were parents surprised or concerned that their child had a male teacher. And they had been advised by colleagues or other staff not to hug children.\nReich-Shapiro and fellow researchers made several recommendations to increase male representation in the field. Low pay has long been acknowledged as a major issue in the early childhood field. Over 70% of male educators who said they intended to stay in the early education workforce noted an increased salary was a major motivating factor for them to commit to the career long-term. The report suggests paying all early childhood educators the way elementary school teachers are paid.\nCities and programs should establish support groups for male early childhood educators and provide mentoring and professional development advice for male educators and their program leaders.\nThe authors also suggest that traditional recruitment approaches for early childhood educators \"do not address the gender gap in the field.\" They recommend providing young men opportunities to work with children through training and volunteer programs, targeting groups of men who are considering a career change, such as fathers.", "54": "Nationwide, only about three percent of early childhood teachers are male in the U.S. Experts say this can have an impact on young children whose understanding of gender roles and identity are rapidly forming. Research has found that having access to diverse teachers is beneficial for children. For the youngest learners, it means they are more likely to get exposed to different varieties of play and communication. It also helps them develop healthy ideas around gender.\n\"In our world and our society, we have very specific stereotypes(模式化形象) of gender roles,\" said Mindi Reich-Shapiro, an assistant professor in the teacher education department of the Borough of Manhattan Community College, and one of the authors of a recent study. \"It's important for children to see other possibilities and other paths they can take.\"\nDespite mostly feeling supported by colleagues and family members, many of the male educators surveyed in the study reported facing social or cultural resistance in their careers as early education teachers. Some also reported that there were parents surprised or concerned that their child had a male teacher. And they had been advised by colleagues or other staff not to hug children.\nReich-Shapiro and fellow researchers made several recommendations to increase male representation in the field. Low pay has long been acknowledged as a major issue in the early childhood field. Over 70% of male educators who said they intended to stay in the early education workforce noted an increased salary was a major motivating factor for them to commit to the career long-term. The report suggests paying all early childhood educators the way elementary school teachers are paid.\nCities and programs should establish support groups for male early childhood educators and provide mentoring and professional development advice for male educators and their program leaders.\nThe authors also suggest that traditional recruitment approaches for early childhood educators \"do not address the gender gap in the field.\" They recommend providing young men opportunities to work with children through training and volunteer programs, targeting groups of men who are considering a career change, such as fathers.", "55": "Nationwide, only about three percent of early childhood teachers are male in the U.S. Experts say this can have an impact on young children whose understanding of gender roles and identity are rapidly forming. Research has found that having access to diverse teachers is beneficial for children. For the youngest learners, it means they are more likely to get exposed to different varieties of play and communication. It also helps them develop healthy ideas around gender.\n\"In our world and our society, we have very specific stereotypes(模式化形象) of gender roles,\" said Mindi Reich-Shapiro, an assistant professor in the teacher education department of the Borough of Manhattan Community College, and one of the authors of a recent study. \"It's important for children to see other possibilities and other paths they can take.\"\nDespite mostly feeling supported by colleagues and family members, many of the male educators surveyed in the study reported facing social or cultural resistance in their careers as early education teachers. Some also reported that there were parents surprised or concerned that their child had a male teacher. And they had been advised by colleagues or other staff not to hug children.\nReich-Shapiro and fellow researchers made several recommendations to increase male representation in the field. Low pay has long been acknowledged as a major issue in the early childhood field. Over 70% of male educators who said they intended to stay in the early education workforce noted an increased salary was a major motivating factor for them to commit to the career long-term. The report suggests paying all early childhood educators the way elementary school teachers are paid.\nCities and programs should establish support groups for male early childhood educators and provide mentoring and professional development advice for male educators and their program leaders.\nThe authors also suggest that traditional recruitment approaches for early childhood educators \"do not address the gender gap in the field.\" They recommend providing young men opportunities to work with children through training and volunteer programs, targeting groups of men who are considering a career change, such as fathers."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What was the woman's problem?", "options": ["A) She was pierced by a chicken bone.", "B) She was coughing all the time.", "C) She suffered from lung cancer.", "D) She suffered from shock."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She was coughing all the time.\n【定位】The unnamed woman, from the province of Shandong, started to have coughing problems\nwhen she was 7 or 8 years old. For 14 years, she made numerous hospital visits. However, no\ndoctor could identify any problem. Her uncontrollable coughing was a mystery.\n这名未透露姓名的女子来自山东省，从七八岁起就开始有咳嗽的毛病。十四年间，她无数次\n跑医院。然而，没有一个医生能查出任何问题。她那无法控制的咳嗽成了一个谜。\n【信号】反复强调的核心症状即答案句。判定她问题是什么，要抓原文反复落在同一件事上的那句。\n第 2 段 started to have coughing problems、Her uncontrollable coughing was a\nmystery 两处都把焦点钉在咳嗽上，第 1 段也以 a persistent cough（持续的咳嗽）开篇\n——多处强调、贯穿始终的那个症状，就是她的核心问题，这就是答案信号。\n【替换】coughing problems ↔ coughing all the time\n同义改写：咳嗽的毛病等于一直在咳\nFor 14 years ↔ all the time\n概括替换：长达 14 年等于一直总是\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 She was pierced by a chicken bone. 借用了原文确实念到的 a piece of chicken\nbone，听到 chicken bone 很容易手一抖就选。但原文说的是鸡骨头卡在肺里，是导致咳\n嗽的原因，而且并非 pierced（刺穿）；题问的是她表现出的问题——咳嗽，不是病因，故\nA 答非所问。\n借用原文：a piece of chicken bone lodged in her lung\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 She suffered from lung cancer. 蹭了原文反复出现的 lung（肺），把肺里有东西夸\n大成肺癌。原文通篇只说咳嗽和鸡骨头，根本没出现 cancer 一词，这是把听到的 lung 顺\n手脑补成重病的陷阱。\n借用原文：lodged in her lung\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 She suffered from shock. 借了原文 was shocked to learn（得知时大为震惊）里的\nshock。但原文的 shocked 指她得知真相时很吃惊这一情绪反应，不是她患的疾病；题问她\n的问题（毛病），故 D 张冠李戴、答非所问。\n借用原文：was shocked to learn", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "How was the woman's problem eventually solved?", "options": ["A) By eating chicken soup daily.", "B) Through regular exercising.", "C) Through a surgical operation.", "D) By using traditional Chinese medicine."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Through a surgical operation.\n【定位】Doctors performed surgery and removed the bone.\n医生做了手术，把那根骨头取了出来。\n【信号】结尾处交代最终处置的动作句。问最终怎么解决，答案信号是叙事收束段里那句表最后\n做了什么的动作句。第 3 段先靠扫描查出病因，紧接 Doctors performed surgery and\nremoved the bone 给出明确处置动作——做手术取骨，这就是解决问题的那一刻，是答\n案句。\n【替换】Doctors performed surgery ↔ Through a surgical operation\n同根改写：做手术等于通过外科手术\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 By eating chicken soup daily. 蹭了原文反复出现的 chicken（来自 a piece of\nchicken bone），把鸡骨头致病偷换成天天喝鸡汤治病。原文根本没提任何喝汤、食疗，这\n是靠 chicken 撞词制造的听到鸡就选陷阱。\n借用原文：a piece of chicken bone\nB) 未提及\n选项 Through regular exercising. 说靠规律锻炼解决，但原文从开头到结尾只字未提运动\n锻炼。它利用的是考生生病要多锻炼的生活常识，诱你用常识替代原文，属于原文未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 By using traditional Chinese medicine. 里的 Chinese 和原文 A 22-year-old\nChinese woman 的 Chinese 撞词，让人以为中国人就用中医。但原文交代的解决手段明明\n是 performed surgery（手术），全篇没提任何中医草药，这是借 Chinese 撞词加常识联\n想的双重陷阱。\n借用原文：A 22-year-old Chinese woman\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 A white sheep named Prickles that ran away from an Australian farm during the 2013 bushfires,\nrecently returned home.\n一只名叫Prickles的白羊在2013年丛林大火期间从澳大利亚一个农场逃跑,最近回家了。\n2 According to farmer Alice Gray, Prickles was only a lamb when she ran away. The bushfires that hit\nthe area back then destroyed a large part of her family's massive property. They thought Prickles had\ndied in the fire. But instead, the young sheep escaped into a 200-acre forest near the farm. Once the\nfires were over, the family had to fix the damage done to the farm, as it was such a large property. This\nincluded rebuilding about 50 kilometers of fencing. It was this huge fencing that prevented Prickles from\nfinding her way back. Over the years, the family spotted her a few times. They even recorded her with\ncameras installed to monitor deer activity. But while they knew Prickles was alive, they couldn't find her\nand never expected her to return by herself. Seven years later, they were proven wrong.\n据农场主Alice Gray说,Prickles逃跑时还只是一只羊羔。当时袭击该地区的丛林大火摧毁了她家大片地产\n的很大一部分。他们以为Prickles在火灾中丧生了。但实际上,这只小羊逃进了农场附近一片200英亩的森\n林。火灾结束后,由于地产面积很大,这家人不得不修复农场遭受的损坏。这包括重建约50公里的围栏。正\n是这道巨大的围栏阻止了Prickles找到回家的路。多年来,这家人看到过她几次。他们甚至用安装来监测鹿\n类活动的相机拍到过她。但尽管他们知道Prickles还活着,却找不到她,也从未料到她会自己回来。七年后,\n事实证明他们错了。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the news report say about the white sheep Prickles?", "options": ["A) It was born 13 years ago.", "B) It was Alice Gray's lovely pet.", "C) It got injured in a big bushfire.", "D) It ran away into a nearby forest."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It ran away into a nearby forest.\n【定位】A white sheep named Prickles that ran away from an Australian farm during the 2013\nbushfires, recently returned home.\n一只名叫 Prickles 的白羊，在 2013 年的山火期间从一座澳大利亚农场逃走，最近回了家。\n【信号】新闻首句即核心事实。新闻短消息的第一句通常就把最关键的事实交代清楚。这里开篇 A\nwhite sheep named Prickles that ran away from an Australian farm during the 2013\nbushfires 直接点明这只羊 \"在山火期间逃走\"，这就是承载第 3 题答案的信号句——顺听到\n第一句就该锁定。\n【替换】ran away from an Australian farm ↔ ran away into a nearby forest\n同一逃走动作的去向补全\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项说羊 It was born 13 years ago.，但录音只提到 \"2013 bushfires\" 这个火灾年份，从\n没说羊出生于 13 年前——它把年份信息张冠李戴到 \"出生\" 上，专骗听到年数就对号入座\n的人。\n借用原文：during the 2013 bushfires\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项称羊是 It was Alice Gray's lovely pet.，录音里 According to farmer Alice Gray 确\n实出现了 Alice Gray，但她是农场主、是消息来源，录音从没说这羊是她的 \"宠物\"——这\n是借真实人名编造的关系。\n借用原文：According to farmer Alice Gray\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项说羊 It got injured in a big bushfire.，\"bushfire\" 是录音反复出现的原词，听到就想\n选；但录音说的是羊 escaped into a 200-acre forest 逃走并活了下来，从没说它在火中受\n伤，\"injured\" 是无中生有。\n借用原文：escaped into a 200-acre forest near the farm", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did the family do after the bushfires?", "options": ["A) They rebuilt the fencing around their farm.", "B) They spent seven years replanting their farm.", "C) They claimed damages for their heavy losses.", "D) They installed a camera to monitor sheep activity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They rebuilt the fencing around their farm.\n【定位】Once the fires were over, the family had to fix the damage done to the farm, as it was such a\nlarge property. This included rebuilding about 50 kilometers of fencing.\n大火一过，这家人就不得不修复农场受到的损坏，因为农场面积太大了。这其中就包括重建\n约 50 公里的围栏。\n【信号】时间信号词 Once the fires were over。答案句由时间信号 Once the fires were over\n（大火一过）引出，正好对上题干的 \"after the bushfires\"。这个时间词就是定位信号——\n它一出现，后面紧跟的就是 \"灾后这家人做的事\"，听到它要立刻进入接答案状态。\n【替换】rebuilding about 50 kilometers of fencing ↔ rebuilt the fencing around their farm\n同一重建围栏动作的概括\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项说 They spent seven years replanting their farm.，\"seven years\" 是录音原词，但\n录音里 Seven years later, they were proven wrong 的七年指的是 \"羊七年后回来\"，不是\n\"花七年补种农场\"，\"replanting\" 录音根本没提，是张冠李戴加无中生有。\n借用原文：Seven years later, they were proven wrong\nC) 未提及\n选项称 They claimed damages for their heavy losses.，录音确实说山火 destroyed a\nlarge part of her family's massive property 造成了损失，听到 \"损失\" 容易顺势联想到\n\"索赔\"，但录音从头到尾没说他们去索赔，\"claimed damages\" 是凭损失情节脑补出来\n的。\n借用原文：destroyed a large part of her family's massive property\nD) 偷换概念\n选项说 They installed a camera to monitor sheep activity.，录音里 cameras installed\nto monitor deer activity 确实有装摄像头，但监控的是 \"deer（鹿）\" 不是 \"sheep\n（羊）\"，选项只改了一个名词就把意思偷换了，专骗听到 \"camera/monitor\" 就选的人。\n借用原文：cameras installed to monitor deer activity", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What is the news report mainly about?", "options": ["A) The disappearance of some gold and diamonds.", "B) The transfer of tons of precious metal by air.", "C) The crash of a Russian cargo airplane.", "D) The loss of gold from an airplane."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） The loss of gold from an airplane.\n【定位】Tons of gold have fallen out of the sky in Russia after a cargo plane malfunctioned in midair\nthis morning.\n今天上午，一架货机在半空中发生故障后，数吨黄金从俄罗斯的天空中坠落下来。\n【信号】新闻首句即点题。新闻短消息的核心动作往往压在开头第一句，本则的首句 Tons of gold\nhave fallen out of the sky in Russia after a cargo plane malfunctioned in midair 一句\n话就交代了\"金子从飞机上掉下来\"，整篇后文都是围着它展开的细节。\n【替换】Tons of gold have fallen out of the sky ↔ The loss of gold from an airplane.\n金子掉落=金子丢失\na cargo plane ↔ from an airplane\n货机就是飞机\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 盗用了录音里的 gold 和 diamonds 两个原词，听着很耳熟，但它说的是 The\ndisappearance of some gold and diamonds.（金子钻石的消失），\"消失\"指不知去向，\n而录音明确是\"从飞机上掉落\"且大部分已被寻回，方向不符，也漏掉了\"飞机\"这个关键。\n借用原文：265 million pounds worth of gold and diamonds\nB) 答非所问\nB 的 The transfer of tons of precious metal by air.（用飞机空运大量贵金属）确实是事情\n的背景，precious metal、by air 也都能在录音里找到影子，但它只描述了\"正在空运\"这个\n前提，完全没提\"出故障、金子掉落\"这个真正的新闻点，以偏概全。\n借用原文：265 million pounds worth of gold and diamonds\nC) 过度推断\nC 说 The crash of a Russian cargo airplane.（俄罗斯货机坠毁），Russian、cargo\nairplane 都是原词，但录音里飞机是 made an emergency landing（紧急迫降）而非坠\n毁，crash 把事故程度夸大了，与事实相反。\n借用原文：the aircraft made an emergency landing in Magan", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What did the aircraft do when the incident happened?", "options": ["A) It made an emergency landing.", "B) It informed the local police at once.", "C) It contacted the goldmine company.", "D) It had a crew member fix the problem."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It made an emergency landing.\n【定位】However, the aircraft made an emergency landing in Magan after it began losing some of its\nvaluable cargo.\n然而，这架飞机在开始丢失部分贵重货物后，于马甘紧急迫降。\n【信号】转折 However 后是落点。本句以转折词 However 起头——前文一直在讲金子掉了、被寻\n回多少，转折之后才交代飞机本身的应对动作 made an emergency landing，转折后的句\n子正是本题答案句。\n【替换】the aircraft made an emergency landing ↔ It made an emergency landing.\n几乎原句对应\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nB 说 It informed the local police at once.（飞机立刻通知了当地警方），police 一词录\n音确实念到了，但录音是 Police have sealed off the runway（警方封锁跑道），是警方主\n动介入，并没有\"飞机通知警方\"这回事，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Police have sealed off the runway\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说 It contacted the goldmine company.（飞机联系了金矿公司），goldmine\ncompany 是原词，但录音只说 Nine tons of gold on plane AN-12 belonged to a\ngoldmine company（飞机上九吨金子归一家金矿公司所有），金矿公司只是货物的主人，\n根本没说飞机去联系它。\n借用原文：belonged to a goldmine company\nD) 未提及\nD 说 It had a crew member fix the problem.（飞机让机组人员修好了故障），录音确实\n提到 malfunctioned（故障）和后文的 technical engineers（技术工程师），听着像\"有人\n修\"，但录音里飞机是迫降处理，从没说让机组成员在空中把问题修好了，纯属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the news report say about the technical engineers who worked on the plane before take-off?", "options": ["A) They will cooperate with the police.", "B) They had checked the plane carefully.", "C) They will be questioned by the police.", "D) They took some gold bars and diamonds."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They will be questioned by the police.\n【定位】Technical engineers who worked on the plane prior to take-off are reportedly going to be\nquestioned by the police.\n据报道，起飞前给这架飞机做过工作的技术工程师将接受警方问询。\n【信号】末段交代相关人员后续。新闻把对涉事人员的后续处置放在结尾，末段 Technical\nengineers ... are reportedly going to be questioned by the police 一句直接点出工程师\n的去向，是本题答案句；且它与题干用的 technical engineers...prior to take-off 几乎同\n词，定位极其明确。\n【替换】are reportedly going to be questioned by the police ↔ They will be questioned by the police.\n将被警方问询，几乎同句\nTechnical engineers who worked on the plane prior to take-off ↔ They\n代词回指工程师\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 说 They will cooperate with the police.（他们将配合警方），police 是原词且\"将来 +\n警方\"的框架很像，但录音是 be questioned by the police（被警方问询，被动），工程师\n是被调查的对象，并非主动配合，主被动方向被偷换。\n借用原文：are reportedly going to be questioned by the police\nB) 未提及\nB 说 They had checked the plane carefully.（他们已仔细检查过飞机），借工程师\nworked on the plane 联想出\"检查\"，但录音只说工程师\"起飞前做过工作\"且\"将被问询\"，\n从没说他们\"仔细检查过\"，更没说检查得是否仔细，无中生有。\nD) 过度推断\nD 说 They took some gold bars and diamonds.（他们拿走了金条和钻石），gold\nbars、diamonds 全是原词，又借录音 it is unclear if it was an accident or an\nattempted robbery（尚不清楚是事故还是抢劫未遂）的联想，把工程师直接坐实成偷窃\n者，但录音只说\"将被问询\"、尚未定性，是过度推断。\n借用原文：it is unclear if it was an accident or an attempted robbery", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why does the man have his conversation with the woman recorded?", "options": ["A) For the company's records.", "B) For future training purposes.", "C) To follow the company's rule.", "D) To ensure information security."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） For future training purposes.\n【定位】I just want to make you aware that this call is being recorded and may be used in the future\nfor training purposes.\n我想提醒您一下，本次通话正在录音，将来可能会用于培训目的。\n【信号】目的状语 for...purposes。答案信号是表目的的短语 used in the future for training\npurposes——\"录音\"这一动作后面紧跟的\"for ... purposes\"就是它的目的，听到\nfor...purposes 这种目的状语，落点即在其后那个理由词上。\n【替换】used in the future for training purposes ↔ For future training purposes.\n几乎原句压缩\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 For the company's records. 里的 records，借自客服后面查单时说的 according to\nour records——那是\"按我们的记录\"，与\"为什么录音\"毫不相干，是听到 records 就想选\n的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：according to our records\nC) 未提及\n选项 To follow the company's rule. 说\"为遵守公司规定\"，录音里客服只说录音用于培训\n目的，从没提\"规定/rule\"这回事，属原文未提及。\nD) 未提及\n选项 To ensure information security. 说\"为保障信息安全\"，虽与客服场景的常识联想很\n近，但录音讲录音原因时只字未提\"信息安全\"，是按常识脑补出来的理由，原文无依据。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is the woman's purpose of calling the furniture company?", "options": ["A) To check her customer reference number.", "B) To inquire about the price of office chairs.", "C) To get her money back for the returned chair.", "D) To make complaints about its customer service."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） To get her money back for the returned chair.\n【定位】I recently bought an office chair from your company, but I returned it about a week ago. I'm\njust calling to inquire when I will be able to get my money back.\n我最近从贵公司买了一把办公椅，但大约一周前退掉了。我打电话就是想问问，我什么时候\n能拿到退款。\n【信号】自报来意 I'm just calling to。答案信号是女方自报来意的句式 I'm just calling to inquire\nwhen I will be able to get my money back——\"I'm (just) calling to...\"后面接的正是来\n电目的，听到这个句式就锁定其后内容。\n【替换】get my money back ↔ get her money back for the returned chair\n人称改写+补宾语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 To check her customer reference number. 搬来客服后面要的 customer reference\nnumber——那是客服为查单向她索取编号，不是她打电话的目的，属听到原词就选的原词\n陷阱。\n借用原文：What's your customer reference number, please?\nB) 未提及\n选项 To inquire about the price of office chairs. 借了 office chair 的熟悉感（录音确有\noffice chair），但她从没问价格，全程围绕退款，属未提及。\n借用原文：I recently bought an office chair\nD) 过度推断\n选项 To make complaints about its customer service. 说\"投诉客服\"，她确实抱怨了退款\n超时 it's been five working days and I haven't received anything，但语气是查询而非投\n诉，且本题问\"来电目的\"是退款，把抱怨语气推成\"投诉\"是过度推断。\n借用原文：it's been five working days and I haven't received anything", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the woman say about her bank card?", "options": ["A) She had to update its information.", "B) She forgot where she had left it.", "C) She lost it about three days ago.", "D) She was issued a new card."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She was issued a new card.\n【定位】Oh, maybe it has something to do with my new card. It was sent to me on Tuesday. Maybe\nthe bank canceled my old one before you've made the payment.\n哦，这可能跟我的新卡有关。新卡周二寄到我这儿了。也许银行在你们付款之前就把我的旧\n卡注销了。\n【信号】她主动提及 my new card。答案信号是女方自己抛出的新信息 my new card——客服说卡\n号有问题后，她解释\"可能跟我的新卡有关，新卡周二寄到\"，这句关于卡的主动陈述就是答\n案句。\n【替换】my new card ↔ She was issued a new card.\n主被动转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 She had to update its information. 里的 update，搬自客服随后的建议 update\nyour card details——那是客服让她去更新，不是她\"说关于卡\"的内容，且发生在后面，属\n张冠李戴式原词陷阱。\n借用原文：update your card details\nB) 未提及\n选项 She forgot where she had left it. 说她忘了卡放哪，录音里她对卡的陈述只有\"换了\n新卡、旧卡可能被注销\"，从没说\"忘了放哪\"，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 She lost it about three days ago. 拼了两处熟悉感：卡的话题里没说\"丢\"，three\ndays 则盗自网站规则 within three working days（退款时限），把\"三个工作日\"嫁接\n成\"三天前丢卡\"，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：within three working days", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the man advise the woman to do?", "options": ["A) Reconsider her options for payment methods.", "B) Make a specific note on the company's system.", "C) Update her bank card details on the company's website.", "D) Upload her personal information to the company's website."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Update her bank card details on the company's website.\n【定位】Well, you'll need to update your card details directly on our website. Just make sure you're\nlogged in. And then under the description of your personal information, you should see an\noption for updating payment methods.\n嗯，您需要直接在我们网站上更新您的卡信息。先确保您已登录。然后在您个人信息那一栏\n下面，您应该能看到一个更新支付方式的选项。\n【信号】建议句 you'll need to。答案信号是建议句式 you'll need to update your card details\ndirectly on our website——\"you'll need to\"是客服给出建议的标志，落点就是其后那个\n动作\"在网站上更新卡信息\"。\n【替换】update your card details directly on our website ↔\nUpdate her bank card details on the company's website\n人称改写+近义等值\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Reconsider her options for payment methods. 盗用原文 an option for updating\npayment methods 里的 option、payment methods，但原文是\"看到一个更新支付方式\n的选项\"，不是\"重新考虑支付方式\"，reconsider 是凭空换上的动作，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：an option for updating payment methods\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 Make a specific note on the company's system. 盗自客服自己说的 I'll make a\nnote here on the system for you——那是客服\"我来做个备注\"，不是建议\"她\"做的，主语\n张冠李戴，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：I'll make a note here on the system for you\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Upload her personal information to the company's website. 把原文 under the\ndescription of your personal information 的 personal information 搬来，又把 update\n偷换成 upload（更新≠上传），原文只让她更新卡信息、没让她上传个人信息，属原词陷\n阱兼偷换概念。\n借用原文：under the description of your personal information", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why isn't the man going to cook?", "options": ["A) He is feeling exhausted.", "B) He is tired of cooking.", "C) He has to work late.", "D) He wants to try Asian foods."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He is feeling exhausted.\n【定位】M: No, sorry, I'm actually pretty worn out. Let's just go out somewhere close to eat.\n男：不了，抱歉，我其实挺累的。我们就近找个地方出去吃吧。\n【信号】被问者对做饭请求的直接应答。上一句女方问 Are you going to prepare a meal for\nus?，男方紧接着应答 I'm actually pretty worn out，这句被问者的直接回答就是答案信\n号，理由就在他亲口给的这句里。\n【替换】worn out ↔ exhausted\nworn out 与 exhausted 是同义替换\n【排除】B) 未提及\n男士只说累，从没说讨厌做饭这件事，He is tired of cooking. 属凭空添加，录音无对应。\nC) 未提及\n现实里累常因为加班，但录音从头到尾没提工作，He has to work late. 是拿常识替文本，\n属未提及。\nD) 答非所问\n录音后面确实聊到印度、越南等亚洲菜，He wants to try Asian foods. 盗用了这层话题，\n但那是点什么外卖，不是他不做饭的原因，答非所问。\n借用原文：How about Indian food?", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the man say he'll do on his phone?", "options": ["A) Book a table.", "B) Order a delivery.", "C) Download a menu.", "D) Locate a restaurant."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Order a delivery.\n【定位】M: Yeah, great. I'll use that food delivery app on my phone.\n男：好啊，太好了。我用手机上那个外卖配送 app。\n【信号】说话人用 I'll 直接表态要做的动作。紧承上句女方问 Can we get a meal delivered?，男方\n应答 I'll use that food delivery app on my phone，I'll 后跟的动作就是答案信号，明确说\n出他要做叫外卖这件事。\n【替换】use that food delivery app ↔ Order a delivery\n用外卖 app 等于点外卖配送\n【排除】A)\nBook a table. 对应出去吃饭订桌，但女方说 I think we should stay in，两人改成在家叫\n外卖，订位的方案被放弃，与原文相反。\n借用原文：I think we should stay in and save some cash.\nC)\n选项 Download a menu. 蹭了用手机 app 的场景，听上去和外卖相关，但录音说的是用\napp 下单，从没说下载菜单，属未提及的脑补。\nD) 答非所问\n后文女方查中餐馆说 it's 15 kilometers away，Locate a restaurant. 盗用了查距离这层，\n但那是后面挑菜时顺带看位置，不是男士在手机上表态要做的事，答非所问。\n借用原文：it's 15 kilometers away", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the woman think of Indian food?", "options": ["A) It is not tasty.", "B) It is not healthy.", "C) It is too oily.", "D) It is too spicy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） It is too spicy.\n【定位】W: That's not the kind of hot I mean. I'm just not a fan of sauces. That made me sweat and\ncry. I need something mild.\n女：那不是我说的那种热乎。我就是不喜欢那种酱料，辣得我又出汗又流泪。我需要清淡\n点的。\n【信号】评价对象后跟出的负面理由与感受。男方提 How about Indian food?，女方应答 made\nme sweat and cry，这句对印度菜的具体负面感受就是答案信号——辣得出汗流泪，正是\n她嫌它的点。\n【替换】That made me sweat and cry ↔ It is too spicy\n辣得出汗流泪即太辣\n【排除】A) It is not tasty. 听上去像负面评价，但女方嫌的是辣，从没说印度菜难吃，属未提及。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 healthy 出现在女方说 How about something healthy?，It is not healthy. 盗用了\n这个词，但她对印度菜的嫌弃是辣，不是不健康，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：How about something healthy?\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音里油腻感对应的是意大利菜 It's a bit heavy 和后文 too fattening，It is too oily. 把对\n别道菜的印象安到印度菜上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：It's a bit heavy", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the man think of the woman?", "options": ["A) She is too concerned about money.", "B) She is too weight-conscious.", "C) She is too picky about food.", "D) She is too eager to please."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） She is too picky about food.\n【定位】M: Oh, you're really difficult to please.\n男：哦，你可真难伺候。\n【信号】评价者忍不住说出的直接总评。在女方一路否掉沙拉、印度菜、墨西哥菜、意大利菜之后，\n男方脱口而出 you're really difficult to please，这句直接总评就是答案信号——他嫌她样\n样不满意、太难伺候。\n【替换】you're really difficult to please ↔ She is too picky about food\n难伺候即对吃太挑剔\n【排除】A)\n录音里钱对应女方说 save some cash，She is too concerned about money. 盗用了省钱\n这层，但那是她的想法，男方对她的评价是难伺候，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：save some cash\nB)\n录音里身材对应女方说 I'm trying to keep a slim figure，She is too weight-conscious.\n盗用了这层，但那是她自己的理由，男方的总评是难伺候，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I'm trying to keep a slim figure\nD) 原词陷阱\nShe is too eager to please. 借用了答案句里的 please，却把方向反了：男方说的是她\ndifficult to please（难被讨好），不是她急着讨好别人，与原文相反。\n借用原文：you're really difficult to please", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn from the new study by the University of Lincoln?", "options": ["A) Owners' personalities affect their cats' behaviour and wellbeing.", "B) Parents' personalities can affect the personalities of their children.", "C) Parents and cat owners alike experience high levels of anxiety.", "D) More and more people are treating pet cats like their children."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题） Owners' personalities affect their cats' behaviour and wellbeing.\n【定位】A new study carried out by the University of Lincoln has found a link between the\npersonalities of cat owners and the behaviour and wellbeing of their cats.\n林肯大学开展的一项新研究发现，猫主人的性格与其猫咪的行为和健康状况之间存在关联。\n【信号】开头点题句即结论。篇章独白的主结论几乎都在第一句，has found a link between the\npersonalities of cat owners and the behaviour and wellbeing of their cats 这一\"研究\n发现了…关联\"的句式就是答案信号，开门见山给出研究主发现。\n【替换】a link between the personalities of cat owners and the behaviour and wellbeing of their cats ↔\nOwners' personalities affect their cats' behaviour and wellbeing.\n\"关联\"被改写成\"影响\"\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里确实念到 a parent's personality can affect the personality of a child，但那只是\n用来打比方说明\"猫和主人也一样\"，研究本身讲的是猫不是孩子；B 把这个比方当成研究发\n现，是典型\"听到原词就选\"。\n借用原文：just as a parent's personality can affect the personality of a child\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段确实念到 high levels of anxiety，C 把\"焦虑\"这个词捡来，硬说\"父母和猫主人\n都有高焦虑\"，但原文只说某些主人焦虑会影响猫，从没说父母也焦虑，是把原词拼成了一\n句没说过的话。\n借用原文：individuals with high levels of anxiety, fear, anger, depression and loneliness\nD) 偷换概念\n录音第 5 段念到 Many owners regard their pets as a family member（很多主人把宠物\n当家人），D 偷换成\"越来越多人把猫当孩子养\"，既加了\"越来越多\"的趋势，又把\"家人\"换\n成\"孩子\"，原文并未讲这是个增长趋势。\n借用原文：Many owners regard their pets as a family member", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the passage say most pet owners want to do?", "options": ["A) Give their pets behavioural training.", "B) Provide their pets with the best care.", "C) Know their pets' feelings and desires.", "D) Interact with their pets in novel ways."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Provide their pets with the best care.\n【定位】The majority of owners want to provide the best care for their pets and it's therefore possible\nthat pets could be affected by the way their owners interact with and manage them.\n绝大多数主人都想为自己的宠物提供最好的照料，因此宠物很可能会受到主人对待和管理它\n们的方式的影响。\n【信号】意愿动词 want to 直接应答。信号是题干钩子词 want to 在原文原样出现：The majority\nof owners want to provide the best care for their pets，want to 后面紧跟的动作就是\n答案，是最直接的细节定位。\n【替换】The majority of owners want to provide the best care for their pets ↔\nProvide their pets with the best care.\n几乎原句保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音反复出现 behavioural styles、behaviours 这类\"行为\"词，A 把它捡成\"behavioural\ntraining（行为训练）\"，但原文从没说主人想给猫做训练，是听到 behavioural 就想选的陷\n阱。\n借用原文：more aggressive and anxious behavioural styles\nC) 答非所问\n原文 the underlying nature of the owner 讲的是主人内在性格驱动宠物状态，C 把它扭\n成\"主人想了解宠物的感受和愿望\"，可原文说的是主人想\"照料\"宠物，不是想\"了解其感\n受\"，方向被换了。\n借用原文：the underlying nature of the owner\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音第 5 段确有 interact with and manage them，D 借走 interact 拼成\"用新颖方式互\n动\"，但原文只说主人会以某种方式对待宠物，从没提\"novel（新颖）\"方式，多加了限定\n词。\n借用原文：the way their owners interact with and manage them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the passage say is still needed to understand the effects of owners' personalities on their pets?", "options": ["A) More convincing explanation.", "B) More extensive sampling.", "C) Collection of more data.", "D) Further investigation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Further investigation.\n【定位】Further research is needed to investigate the causal nature of this relationship and to look at\nhow owners' personalities are directly influencing their pets' behaviour and wellbeing.\n还需要进一步的研究，来探究这种关系的因果本质，并考察主人的性格是如何直接影响宠物\n的行为和健康的。\n【信号】篇末展望句 is needed。信号是篇末的研究展望句式 Further research is needed to\ninvestigate，is needed 与题干 still needed 直接对应，这种\"还需进一步研究\"的收束句正\n是答案落点。\n【替换】Further research is needed to investigate the causal nature of this relationship ↔\nFurther investigation.\nresearch 与 investigate 同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\n原文说的是还需\"进一步研究去探究因果\"，并没说现有解释不够可信、需要\"更有说服力的解\n释\"；A 凭\"研究、解释\"的话题氛围编出一个原文没提的需求。\nB) 未提及\n原文从未提样本大小，B 的 More extensive sampling.（更大范围取样）是借科普研究的\n专业气味硬造的，篇末展望句里根本没有\"样本/取样\"这层意思。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文需要的是宽泛的 Further research（进一步研究），C 的 Collection of more data.\n（收集更多数据）只是研究的一个具体环节，原文并没单指\"收数据\"，C 以偏概全、缩小了\n范围。\n借用原文：Further research is needed to investigate", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What did the new review of studies claim?", "options": ["A) People should do more running than mere walking.", "B) Running is the best exercise for extending one's life.", "C) People should exercise at least 60 minutes every day.", "D) Running is the easiest form of exercise for most people."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Running is the best exercise for extending one's life.\n【定位】The review concluded that on average runners live three years longer than non-runners and\nthat running does more to extend life than any other form of exercise.\n该综述总结道：平均而言，跑步者比不跑步者多活三年，而且在延长寿命方面，跑步比任何\n其他形式的运动都更有效。\n【信号】结论动词 concluded 领出的那句就是答案句。录音里 The review concluded that 用\nconcluded（总结道）这个结论信号词领出综述的正式说法，结论信号后面跟的就是该研究\nclaim 的内容，正是 19 题要的答案句。\n【替换】running does more to extend life than any other form of exercise ↔\nRunning is the best exercise for extending one's life\n比任何其他都强=最好，比较级升级为最高级\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 People should do more running than mere walking. 借了录音反复出现的 running\n来诱选，但综述说的是跑步比其他运动更延寿，从没让人多跑少走，是听到 running 就想勾\n的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：than any other form of exercise\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 People should exercise at least 60 minutes every day. 偷了开头 One 60-minute\nrun 里的 60 分钟，但原文只是举例说一次 60 分钟的跑步，从没说每天至少运动 60 分钟，\n是把听到的数字硬塞进一个原文没有的主张。\n借用原文：One 60-minute run can add seven hours to your life\nD) 未提及\nD 项 Running is the easiest form of exercise for most people. 听上去像常识，但综述比\n的是延寿效果（哪个更长寿），根本没比哪种运动最容易；easiest 是凭印象脑补出来的，\n原文未提。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is one effect that running has on people according to recent research?", "options": ["A) Improving their brain function.", "B) Regulating their breathing rate.", "C) Slowing down their ageing process.", "D) Accelerating their blood circulation."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Improving their brain function.\n【定位】Research published in recent years has shown that running changes your brain and mind in\nsome fascinating ways, from increasing your brain function to regulating your emotions.\n近年发表的研究表明，跑步会以一些奇妙的方式改变你的大脑和心智，从提升你的脑功能到\n调节你的情绪。\n【信号】shown that 引出近期研究结论，且与题干recent research对应。录音 Research\npublished in recent years has shown that 正对应题干里的 according to recent\nresearch，shown that 这一结论信号后列出的效果就是答案来源；其中 increasing your\nbrain function 直指本题。\n【替换】increasing your brain function ↔ Improving their brain function\nincreasing≈improving，brain function原义保留\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 Regulating their breathing rate. 偷了录音里 regulating your emotions 的\nregulating，但原文 regulating 后面跟的是 emotions（情绪），不是 breathing rate（呼\n吸频率）；它把对的动词配上错的对象，是张冠李戴式的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：regulating your emotions\nC) 未提及\nC 项 Slowing down their ageing process. 蹭了前文延长寿命的话题让人误以为有关衰\n老，但讲近期研究效果这句只提了脑功能和情绪，没说延缓衰老；属把别处话题挪过来的混\n淆。\nD) 未提及\nD 项 Accelerating their blood circulation. 是大家凭常识觉得跑步必然会有的效果，但录\n音讲的是改变大脑和心智（脑功能、情绪），完全没提血液循环，属常识代替原文。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What did another study find about the participants after they had a 30-minute jog?", "options": ["A) They found it easy to control their emotions.", "B) They struggled to handle negative emotions.", "C) They were more eager to enjoy a movie.", "D) They were less affected by sad movies."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They were less affected by sad movies.\n【定位】Participants who usually struggled to handle negative emotions were more intensely affected\nby the sad clips, just as you'd expect. But crucially, this was less so if they had completed the\n30-minute jog.\n那些平时难以处理负面情绪的被试，正如你所料，会被悲伤片段影响得更强烈。但关键在\n于，如果他们完成了那 30 分钟的慢跑，这种影响就会小一些。\n【信号】转折But crucially之后才是真结论。录音前半 were more intensely affected by the sad\nclips 是铺垫常态，真正的研究发现在转折词后：But crucially, this was less so if they\nhad completed the 30-minute jog，But crucially 提示这才是关键落点，对应题干问的慢\n跑后的发现。\n【替换】this was less so if they had completed the 30-minute jog ↔\nThey were less affected by sad movies\nless so指回less affected，sad clips=sad movies\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 They struggled to handle negative emotions. 几乎照搬录音 struggled to handle\nnegative emotions 的原话，但那是描述被试平时（usually）的状态，不是慢跑后的发\n现；题干问的是慢跑之后，它答的是慢跑之前，是最毒的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Participants who usually struggled to handle negative emotions\nA) 过度推断\nA 项 They found it easy to control their emotions. 把原文的受影响变小（less\naffected）夸成了能轻松控制情绪（easy to control），原文只说影响减弱，没说他们就能\n掌控情绪，是把程度推过了头。\n借用原文：less vulnerable to the impact of the sad movie\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 They were more eager to enjoy a movie. 借了录音里 movie 这个词，但原文讲的是\n被试被悲伤片段影响的程度，从没说他们更想看电影（eager to enjoy）；是借 movie 一\n词编出原文没有的内容。\n借用原文：showed them clips from a sad movie", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do we learn about the speaker?", "options": ["A) He is a tour guide.", "B) He is a famous architect.", "C) He is a local entrepreneur.", "D) He is the owner of the Hill House."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He is a tour guide.\n【定位】Welcome to the tour of the Hill House.\n欢迎参加希尔之屋的参观。\n【信号】开场欢迎语暴露讲解员身份。答案信号是录音第一句的开场套话 Welcome to the tour of\nthe Hill House.——『欢迎来参观』这种带 tour 的欢迎词，只有带队讲解的人才会说，\n等于自报家门是导游 / 讲解员。讲解类录音的身份信号常藏在开头的欢迎句里，一开口就\n要抓。\n【替换】Welcome to the tour of the Hill House. ↔ He is a tour guide.\n由动作反推身份\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音确实念到 Charles Hill, the famous architect，但 famous architect 说的是设计这栋\n房子的查尔斯·希尔，不是正在讲解的说话人。选项 He is a famous architect. 把建筑师的\n身份安到了说话人头上，是典型的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Charles Hill, the famous architect\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt was designed and built for a local entrepreneur and his family. 里的 local\nentrepreneur（本地企业家）指的是当年请人盖房的客户，与说话人无关。选项 He is a\nlocal entrepreneur. 同样把客户的身份扣到说话人头上。\n借用原文：a local entrepreneur and his family\nD) 未提及\n录音只说房子是为 a local entrepreneur and his family 建的，从头到尾没说说话人是房\n主；带队做 tour 的讲解员通常也不是产权所有者。选项 He is the owner of the Hill\nHouse. 属未提及的臆测，原文无对应依据。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did the architect do before beginning his design?", "options": ["A) He studied the blueprints of other famous buildings.", "B) He inquired about his client's family background.", "C) He observed his client's life and habits.", "D) He took a tour of his client's old home."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He observed his client's life and habits.\n【定位】Prior to beginning his design, the architect spent some time in the client's old home,\nobserving their life and studying their habits.\n在开始设计之前，建筑师在客户的旧居里待了一段时间，观察他们的生活、研究他们的\n习惯。\n【信号】时间状语 Prior to 对上题干 before。答案信号是时间状语 Prior to beginning his design\n——它和题干里的 before beginning his design 几乎一字不差地对应，等于直接告诉你\n『答案就在这句』。听到与题干时间限定词同义的标志词（prior to = before），立刻锁定\n其后的动作。\n【替换】observing their life and studying their habits ↔ He observed his client's life and habits.\n两个并列动作概括为一句\nin the client's old home ↔ his client's\ntheir 与 client's 指同一对象\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音后文确实出现 all the blueprints for both the building and its contents have been\npreserved，blueprints（图纸）这个词被念到了，但那是说『这栋房子自己的图纸被保存\n下来』，并非建筑师设计前去研究『别家名建筑的图纸』。选项 He studied the\nblueprints of other famous buildings. 盗用 blueprints 一词，却凭空加了 other famous\nbuildings，答非所问。\n借用原文：all the blueprints for both the building and its contents have been preserved\nB) 未提及\n录音提到 client 和 family（a local entrepreneur and his family），让 family\nbackground 看着眼熟，但建筑师做的是『观察生活、研究习惯』，从没『inquired about\nfamily background（打听家世背景）』。inquire 这个动作录音根本没说，属未提及。\n借用原文：a local entrepreneur and his family\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说建筑师 spent some time in the client's old home（在旧居里待了一段时间，目的\n是观察生活），并不是去 took a tour of his client's old home（参观一下旧居）。选项盗\n用 old home 一词，把『常驻观察』偷换成『参观一趟』，重点完全跑偏。\n借用原文：spent some time in the client's old home", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What did the architect's client mainly want?", "options": ["A) A house made of timber and brick.", "B) A house with a lot of free space.", "C) A house of the current fashion.", "D) A house of a unique design."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A house of a unique design.\n【定位】The client's main desire was to have a home with the unique design, so the architect was\ngiven complete freedom.\n客户的主要愿望是拥有一栋设计独特的房子，于是建筑师获得了完全的自由。\n【信号】desire 直接点出客户愿望。答案信号是表愿望的词 The client's main desire was——\ndesire（愿望）与题干的 want 同义，直接领出客户到底想要什么。听到 desire / want /\nwish 这类表意愿的词，其后内容往往就是这类需求题的答案。\n【替换】a home with the unique design ↔ A house of a unique design.\nhome 与 house 近义、措辞几乎照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 most large homes were constructed of timber and brick，但这是在说\n『当时大多数大房子』的普遍做法，紧接着用 however 转折说这栋房子是用混凝土造的。\n选项 A house made of timber and brick. 盗用 timber and brick，描述的却是别家房\n子，不是客户想要的这栋。\n借用原文：most large homes were constructed of timber and brick\nB) 未提及\n录音说 the architect was given complete freedom（建筑师获得完全的设计自由），\nfreedom 与选项里的 free space 长得像、听起来也像，但 free space 指『大量空闲空\n间』，录音从没说客户想要宽敞空间。选项 A house with a lot of free space. 是利用\nfreedom / free 的相似制造混淆，属未提及。\n借用原文：the architect was given complete freedom\nC) 与原文相反\n录音明说 The building style is radically different from what was the fashion of the\nperiod（这栋房子的风格与当时的流行式样截然不同）。选项 A house of the current\nfashion. 盗用 fashion 一词，却把意思反了过来——客户要的恰恰是不随流行、独一无二，\n与原文意思相反。\n借用原文：The building style is radically different from what was the fashion of the period", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do we learn about the pieces of furniture in the children's bedrooms?", "options": ["A) They are well preserved and in pretty good shape.", "B) They are copies built to the architect's designs.", "C) They were designed by another architect.", "D) They were badly damaged but restored."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are copies built to the architect's designs.\n【定位】However, those pieces in the children's bedrooms are copies built to the designs of\nthe architect.\n不过，儿童房里的那些家具是按建筑师的图纸做出来的复制品。\n【信号】However 转折锁定儿童房家具真相。答案信号是转折词 However——前一句刚说大多数\n家具是原件、状况好，However 一转，专门交代『儿童房那批是复制品』。转折后正是题\n目问的那个对象的真相，这类题答案常落在转折之后。\n【替换】those pieces in the children's bedrooms are copies built to the designs of the architect ↔\nThey are copies built to the architect's designs.\n措辞几乎照搬，仅语序微调\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音说 Most of the furniture you will see is original and in good condition——『状况\n好、是原件』讲的是『大多数家具』，紧接着 However 才转到儿童房那批是复制品。选项\nThey are well preserved and in pretty good shape. 把『大多数家具』的状态安到了题目\n问的『儿童房家具』头上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Most of the furniture you will see is original and in good condition\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说儿童房家具是 copies built to the designs of the architect（按『这位建筑师』的\n图纸做的复制品），设计者就是本建筑师本人。选项 They were designed by another\narchitect. 却说是『另一位建筑师』设计的，与原文意思相反。\n借用原文：copies built to the designs of the architect\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 we've been able to replace badly damaged furniture，badly damaged\n这个词被说到了，但那是讲『靠保存下来的图纸去更换严重损坏的家具』，并不是说儿童房\n的家具被严重损坏后又修复了。选项 They were badly damaged but restored. 盗用\nbadly damaged，把它扣到儿童房家具上，对象与意思都错了。\n借用原文：we've been able to replace badly damaged furniture", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) exact\n形容词\n【词性槽】the 与名词 day 之间的定语\n【依据】exact day = 确切的那一天，呼应“无法确知”\n上句说 It is commonly believed that ... Shakespeare was born ... on April 23, 1564 只\n是“人们普遍认为”，本句转折 But it is impossible to know the __(26)__ day——“无法知\n道确切的日子”，exact（确切的）与 impossible to know 的语气精确对应。\n【竞争词】G) particular 义近但不及 exact 精准\nparticular（特定的）虽也修饰 day，但本句强调的是“确切与否”（与下段靠受洗日推算呼应），\nexact 才精准击中“无法确知确切日期”的语义；particular 偏“某一特定的”，指向不准，是本空最\n具迷惑性的干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) conclusively\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰过去分词 known 的状语\n【依据】conclusively known = 确凿可考，与上文“生日不可考”形成对照\n本句有转折词 however，与前文“出生日无法确知”相对：Shakespeare's date of death is\n__(27)__ known, however: it was April 23, 1616——卒日则“确凿地为人所知”，\nconclusively（确凿地）与紧接的精确日期 April 23, 1616 完全吻合。\n【竞争词】O) systematically “系统地被知道”不通，且无转折对照义\nsystematically（系统地）修饰 known 读作“系统地被知道”，语义生硬；本句靠 however 与上\n文“无法确知生日”作对照，需要的是表“确凿”的词，systematically 接不上这层对照。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) scarcity\n名词\n【词性槽】指示词 This 后、of 短语前的主语名词\n【依据】scarcity of information 呼应“few details”\n本段开头 there are few surviving details about his life（生平细节极少）正是 This ___ 所\n指，scarcity（匮乏）与 of biographical information 搭成“传记信息的匮乏”，是对 few\ndetails 的同义复指。\n【竞争词】K) quality “质量”不呼应“数量少”\nquality（品质）指好坏，而上文说的是资料“少”（数量问题），quality 方向错位。\nD) emergence “出现”与“资料缺乏”逻辑相反\nemergence（出现）放进“传记信息的出现”与下文“due to 出身低、记载少”的因果不通，且它另\n有归宿（第 33 空）。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) position\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 social 后的名词中心词\n【依据】social position = 社会地位\n下一句 he was not a noble, but the son of a leather trader（不是贵族、而是皮匠之子）\n正是在解释他的“社会地位”，position 与 social 搭成 social position（社会地位），与下\n文出身的说明完全对应。\n【竞争词】K) quality social quality 不指“出身地位”\nquality（品质）与 social 搭出的意思偏“社交素质”，无法解释下文“非贵族、皮匠之子”的出身落\n差，方向不对。\nD) emergence “出现”与 social 不搭、另有归宿\nemergence（出现）放进 social ___ 不成短语，且它真正的位置在第 33 空 his emergence as\na dramatist。\nL) scarcity 已优先归属第 28 空\nscarcity（匮乏）与 social 搭不出意义，且与第 28 空 “___ of biographical information”（资\n料的稀少）天然对应，应留给那个空。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) probably\n副词\n【词性槽】主语与实义动词 attended 之间的状语\n【依据】probably 呼应全文“资料缺乏、只能推测”的基调\n全文反复强调生平不可考（there are few surviving details about his life），\nShakespeare __(30)__ attended the grammar school 这里用 probably（很可能）表“据\n推测他上过文法学校”，与下文 he would have studied Latin 的虚拟推测语气一致。\n【竞争词】C) conclusively “确凿地”与“资料缺乏只能推测”矛盾\nconclusively（确凿无疑地）表肯定无疑，与上下文“记载稀少、多靠推断”及 would have\nstudied 的推测语气直接冲突，且它另有更贴的归宿（第 27 空 conclusively known）。\nO) systematically “系统地上学”不合逻辑\nsystematically（系统地）修饰 attended（上学）读作“系统地上过文法学校”，语义生硬、不合\n常理，与句意无关。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 C) —— probably 与 conclusively 易填反。conclusively 词性同为\n副词、也能修饰 attended，看着也通；但第 30 空 he would have studied Latin 是推测\n语气，全文又说资料缺乏，须填 probably，把 conclusively 留给第 27 空——防坑靠认准\n全文“推测”基调。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) classical\n形容词\n【词性槽】read 的宾语 literature 前缺定语\n【依据】classical literature 是固定搭配“古典文学”\n上文 he would have studied Latin（学拉丁语）正是古希腊罗马古典教育的标志，\nclassical 与 literature 搭配成“古典文学”，与学拉丁语的语境严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】E) exact exact literature 搭配不成立\nexact 意为“确切的”，没有“确切的文学”这种说法，且它另有更贴的归宿（第 26 空\nexact day）。\nG) particular 语义空泛、与学拉丁语无呼应\nparticular（特定的）虽语法可修饰 literature，但上下文并未限定“某一种”文学，与 Latin 也无\n逻辑呼应，远不如 classical 精准。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) senior\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 was 后作表语\n【依据】eight years his senior = 比他大八岁\nmarried Anne Hathaway, who was eight years his __(32)__ 配 senior 构成英语固定表达\n“eight years one's senior”（年长某人八岁），与下文夫妻育有子女的叙述完全吻合。\n【竞争词】E) exact 不能与 years 构成年龄比较\nexact（确切的）无法说“比他确切八岁”，不构成比较关系，语义不通。\nG) particular 无“年长”义，框架对不上\nparticular（特定的）同样无法填进 “eight years his ___” 表达年龄差，逻辑指向不对。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) emergence\n名词\n【词性槽】物主代词 Shakespeare's 后的名词\n【依据】emergence as a dramatist = 作为剧作家的崭露头角\nShakespeare's __(33)__ as a dramatist in London in the early 1590s 讲他在伦敦“崭露头\n角成为剧作家”这一时间节点，emergence（出现、崭露）与 as a dramatist 搭配，正好填\n补“双胞胎出生”到“成名”之间这段空白。\n【竞争词】K) quality “品质 as a dramatist”逻辑不通\nquality（品质）说 “Shakespeare's quality as a dramatist in the early 1590s” 与“Nothing is\nknown of the period between … and …”所要求的“一个时间事件”对不上，本句要的是一个事\n件节点而非抽象品质。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) captured\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 he 后缺谓语动词\n【依据】capture emotions = （用文字）捕捉、刻画情感\nhe __(34)__ the full range of human emotions and conflicts with a precision that\nremains sharp today，captured（捕捉、刻画）与 emotions 搭成“捕捉人类情感”，正契\n合“用文字精准刻画情感”的赞颂，与后半句 precision/sharp 一脉相承。\n【竞争词】F) generated “生成情感”搭配与语义都不当\ngenerated（产生、生成）说 “generated human emotions” 意为“制造出情感”，但莎翁是“描\n绘、捕捉”既有的人类情感而非“凭空生成”，与 precision/sharp 所赞的“刻画之精准”指向不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) captured", "B) classical", "C) conclusively", "D) emergence", "E) exact", "F) generated", "G) particular", "H) position", "I) precision", "J) probably", "K) quality", "L) scarcity", "M) senior", "N) separated", "O) systematically"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) precision\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 with + 冠词 a 之后\n【依据】与 sharp 呼应，指写作的“精准”\nhe __(34)__ the full range of human emotions and conflicts with a __(35)__ that\nremains sharp today，说他以一种“至今依然锐利的 ___”刻画人类情感，precision（精\n准）正好与 sharp（敏锐、犀利）语义共振，指莎翁文字的精准笔力。\n【竞争词】K) quality 义太泛，与 sharp 不直接呼应\nquality（品质）过于笼统，说 “a quality that remains sharp” 远不如 precision 与 sharp 贴\n合，缺少“精准锐利”的指向性，是最大干扰。\nD) emergence “出现”无法被 sharp 修饰\nemergence（出现）说 “a emergence that remains sharp” 既不搭也不通，且它另有归宿（第\n33 空 emergence as a dramatist）。\nH) position “地位”与 sharp 不搭\nposition（地位）说“依然锐利的地位”讲不通，本词的真正归宿是第 29 空 social position。\nL) scarcity “稀少”与赞美语气相反\nscarcity（匮乏）是贬义、表少，本句在称赞莎翁笔力，感情色彩相反，且它另有归宿（第 28 空\nscarcity of information）。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— quality 冒充 precision。quality 词性对、放进 with a\n__(35)__ that remains sharp today 读着也顺，最具迷惑性；但 sharp 指“精准”，唯\nprecision 与之同向，quality 太空泛——防坑靠盯紧修饰语 sharp 的语义方向。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Pirson claims that some ordinary things may often prove to be miraculously interesting.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】专名 Pirson + 关键词 miraculously / ordinary\n【定位】\"Often, the 'boring things' may not be boring at all. Maybe they are actually little miracles,\"\nhe says.\n「常常，那些『无聊的事』可能根本不无聊。也许它们其实是一个个小小的奇迹，」他说。\n【改写】some ordinary things may often prove to be miraculously interesting ↔\nthe 'boring things' may not be boring at all. Maybe they are actually little miracles\nordinary ↔ boring things；miraculously interesting ↔ little miracles\nPirson claims ↔ he says\n段首 Pirson + 段内 he says 锁说话人\n【辨邻】 D 本段虽出现 what makes us interesting，但落点是 sharing our experiences in\nan authentic way——「真实分享」，并未出现题干所要的「平凡事 ≈ 奇迹」这一\n断言。\nsharing our experiences in an authentic way to connect with other people is what makes\nus interesting\nE 本段核心是 making up some conversation that might be interesting 多半会冷\n场，论述对象是「编出来的对话」，不是「平凡事」。\nIt's going to feel like a made-up conversation that people don't necessarily want to tune in to", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "To make a conversation interesting, it is important that you listen to the other party attentively.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】认真听对方说话\n【定位】Paying attention to the listener is an important part of having a conversation that's interesting\nto both parties.\n关注听者是让一段对话对双方都有趣的重要环节。\n【改写】listen to the other party attentively ↔ Paying attention to the listener\n动副短语 → 动名短语的等值改写\nTo make a conversation interesting ↔ having a conversation that's interesting to both parties\n目的 ↔ 状态 的同义换形\n【辨邻】 K If the listener is not paying attention, it's your sign to shorten the story 是「if 怎\n样就怎么做」的策略指导；题干 it is important that you listen… 强调的是重要性论\n断，正出自 J 段而非 K 段。\nIf the listener is not paying attention, it's your sign to shorten the story or change direction", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "A person who is unable to stimulate others' curiosity or make their life enjoyable may appear somewhat boring.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】激不起好奇 / 点不亮别人 → 显得无趣\n【定位】If you don't arouse someone's curiosity or brighten someone's day, you probably come across\nas being a little bit dull.\n如果你激不起别人的好奇心、点不亮别人的一天，你大概会显得有些乏味。\n【改写】unable to stimulate others' curiosity ↔ If you don't arouse someone's curiosity\nstimulate ↔ arouse 的高频同义替换\nmake their life enjoyable ↔ brighten someone's day\n比喻表达 ↔ 字面表达 的等值\nappear somewhat boring ↔ come across as being a little bit dull\nappear ↔ come across as；boring ↔ dull\n【辨邻】 A too much of a routine can also make you incredibly boring 的原因是 routine，而\n非「无法激起好奇」；题干必备的 curiosity 一词在 A 段未出现。\ntoo much of a routine can also make you incredibly boring", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Interesting people usually possess certain unique qualities, according to a TV program producer.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】TV program producer + unique qualities\n【定位】\"Having a strong point of view, signature style, or being a super-enthusiast in a particular\nfield makes someone interesting,\" she says.\n「拥有鲜明的观点、招牌般的风格，或者在某个领域里是超级爱好者，会让一个人显得有\n趣，」她说。\n【改写】a TV program producer ↔ executive producer of NBC's The Voice\n上位身份 ↔ 具体头衔\npossess certain unique qualities ↔\nHaving a strong point of view, signature style, or being a super-enthusiast in a particular field\n概括词 ↔ 列举式具象表达\n【辨邻】 L Being relatable is also essential、Being authentic, honest, and vulnerable is\nalways interesting 强调「可亲近 / 真实 / 示弱」，这些都是「共性」而非「独特\n性」；与题干 unique qualities 的方向相反。\nBeing relatable is also essential", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Be interested in others and they are sure to be interested in you.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】你对别人感兴趣，别人也对你感兴趣（双向对等）\n【定位】Show some interest in them and they will definitely show some in you.\n对他们表现出一些兴趣，他们也一定会对你表现出一些兴趣。\n【改写】Be interested in others ↔ Show some interest in them\nbe interested in ↔ show interest in\nthey are sure to be interested in you ↔ they will definitely show some in you\nbe sure to ↔ will definitely\n【辨邻】 K get others to talk about themselves 落点是「让我自己显得是 sparkling\nconversation partner」的单向收益，并不主张「他们也会对你 interested」这种对等\n回报。\none strategy to leave others with the impression that you're a sparkling conversation partner is to get\nothers to talk about themselves", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The author considers himself usually good at conducting conversations.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作者自评擅长对话\n【定位】Ordinarily, I think I'm a good conversationalist.\n平时，我自认为是个不错的健谈者。\n【改写】The author considers himself usually good at conducting conversations ↔\nOrdinarily, I think I'm a good conversationalist\n派生名词 ↔ 短语 的等值\n【辨邻】 D Have I become boring? 是质疑而非肯定；本段甚至承接 C 的失语经历，与题干「自\n认为擅长」相反。\nHave I become boring?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Interesting people are always full of curiosity.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】有趣的人 = 始终好奇\n【定位】People who are interesting are persistently curious, she says.\n她说，有趣的人是持续地保有好奇心的。\n【改写】Interesting people are always full of curiosity ↔\nPeople who are interesting are persistently curious\nalways ↔ persistently；full of curiosity ↔ curious\n【辨邻】 H Having that sort of curiosity is almost like a protective gear from getting into\nboredom 是「保持好奇能防无聊」的功能性论述，而非「有趣的人 = 持续好奇」的直\n接定义。\nHaving that sort of curiosity is almost like a protective gear from getting into boredom", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Falling into a routine can turn a person into an utter bore.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】陷入 routine → 变成 utter bore\n【定位】However, too much of a routine can also make you incredibly boring.\n然而，过多的惯例也会让你变得极度乏味。\n【改写】Falling into a routine ↔ too much of a routine\n「陷入」隐喻 ↔ 「过多」字面\nturn a person into an utter bore ↔ make you incredibly boring\nturn into ↔ make；utter bore ↔ incredibly boring\n【辨邻】 B If you don't arouse someone's curiosity or brighten someone's day, you\nprobably come across as being a little bit dull 论的是好奇心维度而非 routine 维\n度，题干必备的 routine 一词在 B 段不存在。\nIf you don't arouse someone's curiosity or brighten someone's day, you probably come across as\nbeing a little bit dull", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "One strategy to be a good conversationalist is to motivate your partner to tell their own stories.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】策略 = 让对方讲他们自己的故事\n【定位】So one strategy to leave others with the impression that you're a sparkling conversation\npartner is to get others to talk about themselves.\n因此，让别人觉得你是个出色的对话伙伴的一种策略，就是让别人去谈他们自己。\n【改写】motivate your partner to tell their own stories ↔ get others to talk about themselves\nmotivate ↔ get；tell their own stories ↔ talk about themselves\nto be a good conversationalist ↔\nto leave others with the impression that you're a sparkling conversation partner\ngood conversationalist ↔ sparkling conversation partner\n【辨邻】 J Paying attention to the listener is an important part of having a conversation\nthat's interesting to both parties 是原则陈述，未触及题干所要的「motivate your\npartner to tell their own stories」这一具体行为。\nPaying attention to the listener is an important part of having a conversation that's interesting to\nboth parties", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Interesting as it might appear, a made-up conversation will probably turn out to be dull.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】编出来的对话最终乏味\n【定位】\"If someone is making up some conversation that might be interesting, it's probably not going\nto land well,\" says Pirson.\n「如果有人编造一段看上去可能有趣的对话，多半收不到好效果，」Pirson 说。\n【改写】Interesting as it might appear, a made-up conversation ↔\nmaking up some conversation that might be interesting\n倒装让步 ↔ 直陈让步\nwill probably turn out to be dull ↔ it's probably not going to land well\nturn out to be dull ↔ not going to land well\n【辨邻】 D The hesitation I felt in not sharing the ordinary things that were happening in\nmy life, and the wild mental search for something more interesting, may have\nbackfired 谈的是作者自身的犹豫与硬找，不是「编造对话整体的冷场效果」。\nThe hesitation I felt in not sharing the ordinary things that were happening in my life, and the\nwild mental search for something more interesting, may have backfired and made me seem\nless interesting", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does Professor John Wilding argue about obesity?", "options": ["A) Its impact on society is expected to rise.", "B) It is now too widespread to be neglected.", "C) It should be regarded as a genetic disease.", "D) Its dictionary definition should be updated."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does Professor John Wilding argue about obesity? 问的是这位教授关于肥胖的**\n主张**，答案要在原文的明确表述中找，不需要跨段综合，是典型的细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does Professor John Wilding argue about obesity? 是标准的 what + 主语\n+ 动词 + 介词短语 结构，主语「Professor John Wilding」，谓语「argue」，「about\nobesity」是 argue 的话题状语；句子平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】He points out that more than 200 genes influence weight. \"Thus body weight is strongly\ninfluenced by biology—it is not an individual's fault if they develop obesity.\"\n他指出有超过 200 个基因影响体重。「因此体重受生物学因素强烈影响——一个人如果得\n了肥胖症，并不是他自己的过错。」\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴：把背景数据安到具名人物头上\nIts impact on society is expected to rise. 实际上是文章开头那句 obesity now\naffecting 29% of the population in England, and expected to rise to 35% by\n2030 的概括，是作者交代的背景数据，并非 Wilding 教授「argue about obesity」的\n主张。\nB) 无中生有：常识合理但原文未支撑\nIt is now too widespread to be neglected. 是个看似合理的概括，但 Wilding 全文论\n证的是「肥胖应被视为疾病」「不是个人过错」，从未以「太普遍以至于无法忽视」为\n论点；原文虽提到 29%/35% 的数据，但落点是「该不该承认为疾病」，与「too\nwidespread to be neglected」不构成等值。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmore than 200 genes influence weight ↔ a genetic disease\nnot an individual's fault if they develop obesity ↔ regarded as a genetic disease\n预判抓住「肥胖与基因强相关 + 应作为疾病看待」两个要点，It should be regarded\nas a genetic disease. 恰好同时覆盖这两点，与 Wilding 在 P1 的主张完全对齐。\nD) 偷换概念：动词替换改变命题\nIts dictionary definition should be updated. 偷换了原文的「meets the dictionary\ndefinition」——Wilding 是说「肥胖**符合**字典关于 disease 的定义」，主张承认它\n**是**疾病，不是主张**更新**字典定义；选项把「符合定义」偷换成「更新定义」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is the popular view of obesity?", "options": ["A) It is difficult to define.", "B) It is a modern disease.", "C) It has much to do with one's genes.", "D) It results from a lack of self-control."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the popular view of obesity? 问的是「大众对肥胖的看法是什么」，要找的是原\n文中对大众观点的客观陈述，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What is the popular view of obesity? 是简单的 what is + 名词短语 结构，「of\nobesity」是 popular view 的修饰，限定看法的对象；结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Yet the widespread view is that obesity is self-induced and that it is entirely the individual's\nresponsibility to do something about it.\n然而，普遍的观点认为，肥胖是自找的，并且完全是个人自己的责任去采取行动加以解决。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：抓住选项与原文方向相反的反例\nIt is difficult to define. 关于「肥胖难以定义」的说法在原文找不到任何依据；原文 P1\n明确给出了肥胖的定义（excess body fat …），反而是「易定义」的方向，与本选项\n相反。\nB) 答非所问：与题干所问对象不对齐\nIt is a modern disease. 想说「肥胖是一种现代疾病」，但原文 the widespread view\n描述的大众观点是「自找的、个人责任」，从未把大众观点概括为「现代疾病」；而且\n大众观点恰恰是「不视为疾病」，与「modern disease」方向冲突。\nC) 张冠李戴：用对立方观点冒充本方观点\nIt has much to do with one's genes. 实际上是 Wilding 教授的主张（more than 200\ngenes influence weight），而非大众观点；原文用 Yet 把大众观点与 Wilding 主张明\n确切开——大众恰好是不认基因这一论的。\nD) ✓ 正确\nobesity is self-induced ↔ results from a lack of self-control\nit is entirely the individual's responsibility to do something about it ↔ a lack of self-control\n预判要点「自找的 + 自己负责」分别对应到 results from a lack of self-control 中的\n「self-control」与「lack of」，D 是唯一同时覆盖这两点的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why are some people opposed to labelling obesity as a disease?", "options": ["A) Obese people would not feel responsible to take any action.", "B) Obese people would not be able to afford the medical costs.", "C) Obese people would be overwhelmed with anxiety.", "D) Obese people would be discriminated against."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why are some people opposed to labelling obesity as a disease? 问的是反方反对的具\n体原因，对应原文反方论据的明确陈述，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干 Why are some people opposed to labelling obesity as a disease? 主干是 why +\nbe + 主语 + 过去分词作表语 (opposed to)，labelling … as a disease 是动名词短语作 to\n的宾语；结构稍长但平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】But labelling obesity as a disease \"risks reducing autonomy, disempowering and robbing\npeople of the intrinsic motivation that is such an important enabler of change.\"\n但把肥胖定性为疾病「有可能削弱人的自主性，让人失去权能感，并夺走对改变如此重要的\n内在动力。」\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nreducing autonomy, disempowering and robbing people of the intrinsic motivation ↔\nnot feel responsible to take any action\nother common diseases, such as high blood pressure and diabetes, require people to take action\nto manage their condition\n↔ to take any action\n预判要点「失去自主与改变动力」在四个选项里只有 Obese people would not feel\nresponsible to take any action. 能落地——「失去内在动力 + 失去自主」≈「不觉得\n自己有责任行动」。\nB) 无中生有：把次相关话题拉来当依据\nObese people would not be able to afford the medical costs. 关于「肥胖者支付不\n起医疗费用」在原文找不到任何对应；原文末段提到的费用相关内容是 health\ninsurance and clinical guidelines promote treatment with drugs and surgery，谈\n的是谁受益（机构与药企），不是谁付不起。\nC) 偷换概念：把动力问题偷换为情绪问题\nObese people would be overwhelmed with anxiety. 把原文「失去 intrinsic\nmotivation」（失去改变的内在动力）偷换为「被焦虑压垮」（情绪问题）——前者关\n乎主体动力是否减弱，后者关乎情绪是否上升，方向不一致。\nD) 张冠李戴：把正方支持理由当反方反对理由\nObese people would be discriminated against. 实际上是 Wilding 在 should help\nreduce the stigma and discrimination experienced by many people with obesity\n提到的——把肥胖视为疾病可以**减少**歧视，是 Wilding 支持贴标签的理由；本题问\n的是**反对**贴标签的理由，正反阵营完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Dr. Richard Pile think of the dictionary definition of disease?", "options": ["A) It is of no use in understanding obesity.", "B) It is too inclusive and thus lacks clarity.", "C) It helps little to solve patients' problems.", "D) It matters little to the debate over obesity."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does Dr. Richard Pile think of the dictionary definition of disease? 问的是具名人\n物对一个具体概念的看法，原文中有他直接的评价句，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does Dr. Richard Pile think of the dictionary definition of disease? 主干是\nwhat + do/does + 主语 + think of + 宾语，think of 是固定搭配「对……的看法」；结构\n常规，无陷阱。\n【定位】He believes that the dictionary definition of disease \"is so vague that we can classify almost\nanything as a disease\" and says the question is not whether we can, but whether we should,\nand to what end.\n他认为，对 disease 一词的字典定义「过于含糊，以至于我们几乎可以把任何东西都归入疾\n病」，并说问题不在于「我们能不能」，而在于「我们该不该，以及目的何在」。\n【选项】A) 过度推断：把局部批评放大成全盘否定\nIt is of no use in understanding obesity. 把 Pile 的「定义含糊」推得过远——他只\n是说定义太宽泛而已，没说「字典定义对理解肥胖毫无用处」；恰恰相反，他的论证起\n点正是「按字典定义看，肥胖确实可被归入 disease」（the question is not whether\nwe can）。\nB) ✓ 正确\nis so vague that we can classify almost anything as a disease ↔\ntoo inclusive and thus lacks clarity\nis so vague that we can classify almost anything as a disease ↔ and thus\n预判要点「过于含糊 + 范围过广」在选项里精准对位为 too inclusive and thus lacks\nclarity，是唯一覆盖两个语义点的选项。\nC) 答非所问：偷偷把问域换到下一步\nIt helps little to solve patients' problems. 谈的是「字典定义对解决患者问题帮助不\n大」，把焦点从「定义本身的特性」偏移到「定义对患者的影响」；题干 What does\nDr. Richard Pile think of the dictionary definition of disease? 问的是 Pile 对**定义\n本身**的看法。\nD) 与原文相反：极性反转\nIt matters little to the debate over obesity. 说「字典定义对肥胖之争没什么关系」，\n但 Pile 在 P3 整段都在围绕字典定义展开批评，并以此为立论起点（the dictionary\ndefinition of disease is so vague）；可见字典定义对他**至关重要**，与「matters\nlittle」语义相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is Dr. Richard Pile's concern about classifying obesity as a disease?", "options": ["A) It may affect obese people's quality of life.", "B) It may accelerate the spread of obesity.", "C) It may cause a shortage of doctors.", "D) It may do little good to patients."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is Dr. Richard Pile's concern about classifying obesity as a disease? 问的是 Pile\n担忧的具体内容，原文末段有明确陈述，属于细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What is Dr. Richard Pile's concern about classifying obesity as a disease? 主干是\nwhat + be + 主语，「about classifying obesity as a disease」是 concern 的修饰短\n语，限定担忧的对象；结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】What's more, making obesity a disease \"may not benefit patients, but it will benefit healthcare\nproviders and the pharmaceutical industry when health insurance and clinical guidelines\npromote treatment with drugs and surgery,\" he warns.\n此外，他警告说，把肥胖变成一种疾病「未必使患者受益，但当医保和临床指南推动用药物\n和手术进行治疗时，它将让医疗机构和制药业受益」。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：用合理却未现的概念顶替\nIt may affect obese people's quality of life. 关于「影响肥胖者生活质量」在原文找不\n到对应表述；Pile 的担忧落在「医疗效用」和「自主与动力」上，不是「生活质量」这\n个独立维度。\nB) 与原文相反/混搭：把背景趋势伪装成反方担忧\nIt may accelerate the spread of obesity. 说贴疾病标签会「加速肥胖蔓延」，但原文\nPile 担忧的是「会让人失去改变动力」（robbing people of the intrinsic\nmotivation）——这是「不利于改善」的间接后果，但与「加速蔓延」是两码事；而且\nP1 中肥胖的扩张趋势是 Wilding 立场的背景数据，与 Pile 的担忧无关。\nC) 无中生有：用相邻话题词凭空造命题\nIt may cause a shortage of doctors. 关于「导致医生短缺」在原文找不到任何依据；\n原文末段提到 healthcare providers and the pharmaceutical industry 会受益，恰恰\n是「医疗供给方得利」的方向，与「短缺」相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmay not benefit patients ↔ may do little good to patients\nmay not benefit patients, but it will benefit healthcare providers and the pharmaceutical industry\n↔ do little good to patients\n四个选项里只有 It may do little good to patients. 在主体（患者）与极性（益处不\n大）两端都与 Pile 的核心担忧一致；其余选项要么换了担忧对象，要么改了担忧内容。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn from the first paragraph about early childhood education in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) It helps raise children's awareness of gender roles.", "B) It exposes children to different ways of interaction.", "C) It is negatively impacted by a lack of male teachers.", "D) It clearly aims to form children's identity through play."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the first paragraph about early childhood education in the\nU.S.?是典型的「从某段我们了解到什么」句式，要的是段内已写明的客观信息，而非推断或\n主旨。\n【拆句】题干What do we learn from the first paragraph about early childhood education in\nthe U.S.?是个特殊疑问句，主干是 What do we learn from the first paragraph，about\nearly childhood education in the U.S. 是 learn 的话题补足，整体平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】Nationwide, only about three percent of early childhood teachers are male in the U.S. Experts\nsay this can have an impact on young children whose understanding of gender roles and\nidentity are rapidly forming.\n全美范围内，幼教队伍中男性仅约百分之三。专家说这会对处于性别角色与身份认知快速形\n成期的幼儿产生影响。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt helps raise children's awareness of gender roles.说幼教「帮助提升」孩子的性别\n角色意识，但原文只说男教师缺失「影响」孩子正在形成的性别角色认知，并未说幼教\n在主动培养这种意识，把「被动塑造期」误读为「主动培养」。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt exposes children to different ways of interaction.说的是文中「师资多样性」才能\n带来的好处，原文限定语是having access to diverse teachers，而题干问的是 early\nchildhood education 整体——把条件子句的局部好处当成整体属性，是张冠李戴。\nC) ✓ 正确\nonly about three percent of early childhood teachers are male ↔ a lack of male teachers\nthis can have an impact on young children ↔ negatively impacted\n预判是「男教师缺失带来负面影响」，It is negatively impacted by a lack of male\nteachers.正好把「缺失」与「负面影响」两块信息都点到了，是定位句的浓缩同义版。\nD) 过度推断\nIt clearly aims to form children's identity through play.说幼教「明确以通过游戏塑造\n孩子身份为目标」，原文只提到孩子的性别角色与身份处在「快速形成期」，并未说幼\n教把这当成目标，且 through play 只是different varieties of play and\ncommunication里的一部分，被拿来当整体目标。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does Mindi Reich-Shapiro emphasize in her comment on childhood education?", "options": ["A) The importance of broadening children's horizons.", "B) The responsibilities of fathers for children's growth.", "C) The urgency of creating teacher education programs.", "D) The role of teachers in motivating children to learn."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does Mindi Reich-Shapiro emphasize in her comment on childhood\neducation?问的是某具体人物在评论中强调的内容，答案就在她说的话里，不是全篇主旨。\n【拆句】题干What does Mindi Reich-Shapiro emphasize in her comment on childhood\neducation?主干是 What does Mindi Reich-Shapiro emphasize，in her comment on\nchildhood education 是状语限定语境，结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】It's important for children to see other possibilities and other paths they can take.\n对孩子来说，看到其他可能性、看到他们可以走的其他道路，是很重要的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsee other possibilities and other paths they can take ↔ broadening children's horizons\nIt's important for children ↔ The importance of\n预判强调「让孩子看到其他可能性」，The importance of broadening children's\nhorizons.正是这句话的抽象浓缩，二者覆盖范围完全重合。\nB) 张冠李戴\nThe responsibilities of fathers for children's growth.说的是父亲的责任，但 Reich-\nShapiro 在P2里通篇没提父亲——把同一篇文章后段such as fathers里出现的「父亲」\n嫁接到她头上，是张冠李戴。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe urgency of creating teacher education programs.谈的是建立教师教育项目的紧\n迫性，原文里 Reich-Shapiro 只是在the teacher education department任职，但她在\nP2里强调的是孩子的认知拓展，而非项目建设——利用她的所属系名做诱饵。\nD) 过度推断\nThe role of teachers in motivating children to learn.说的是教师在激励孩子学习中的\n作用，但原文里 Reich-Shapiro 谈的是让孩子看到不同可能，根本没提到学习动机或激\n励——把「看到可能」过度推断成「激励学习」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn about male teachers from their responses in the study?", "options": ["A) Some of them find it awkward when hugging children.", "B) They feel pressured to keep up with female colleagues.", "C) They find it hard to meet the expectations of kids' parents.", "D) Many of them feel prejudiced against socially and culturally."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about male teachers from their responses in the study?要的是受访\n男教师在调查中说了什么，是研究汇报里的显性细节。\n【拆句】题干What do we learn about male teachers from their responses in the study?主干是\nWhat do we learn about male teachers，from their responses in the study 是介词短\n语状语限定信息来源，整句结构平直、无陷阱。\n【定位】Despite mostly feeling supported by colleagues and family members, many of the male\neducators surveyed in the study reported facing social or cultural resistance in their careers as\nearly education teachers.\n尽管大多数人觉得获得了同事与家人的支持，许多受访男幼教仍然反映在职业中遇到社会或\n文化层面的阻力。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nSome of them find it awkward when hugging children.说的是男教师自己觉得拥抱\n孩子尴尬；原文是they had been advised by colleagues or other staff not to hug\nchildren——是同事的建议，不是男教师本人的主观感受。\nB) 无中生有\nThey feel pressured to keep up with female colleagues.说他们感到要「追赶女同\n事」的压力，但原文从未做男女教师之间的对照，更没说要 keep up with 女同事——\n属于无中生有。\nC) 过度推断\nThey find it hard to meet the expectations of kids' parents.说男教师「难以达到家\n长期望」，但原文只说有些家长surprised or concerned孩子有男教师，并没说家长对\n他们提出了某种「期望」，更没说他们「达不到」——是过度推断。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmany of the male educators ↔ Many of them\nfacing social or cultural resistance ↔ feel prejudiced against socially and culturally\n预判直击「许多男教师感到社会文化层面的不接纳」，Many of them feel prejudiced\nagainst socially and culturally.在主体（many of them）、情感（feel prejudiced\nagainst）、范围（socially and culturally）三块都完全咬合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is needed for men to commit to early childhood education?", "options": ["A) Higher pay.", "B) Job security.", "C) Social recognition.", "D) Better working conditions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is needed for men to commit to early childhood education?问的是文章显性提到\n的留任条件，答案是原文中具体写出的因素。\n【拆句】题干What is needed for men to commit to early childhood education?主干是 What is\nneeded，for men to commit to early childhood education 是不定式短语作状语表「为\n了…」，结构简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】Over 70% of male educators who said they intended to stay in the early education workforce\nnoted an increased salary was a major motivating factor for them to commit to the career\nlong-term.\n在那些表示打算留在幼教队伍的男教师里，超过百分之七十的人指出涨薪是他们长期投身这\n一职业的主要动因。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nan increased salary ↔ Higher pay\na major motivating factor for them to commit to the career long-term ↔\nWhat is needed for men to commit to early childhood education?\n预判明确指向「涨薪」，Higher pay.就是这一信息最直接、最简洁的同义版本，没有附\n加任何原文未明的内容。\nB) 无中生有\nJob security.说的是工作稳定性或保障，原文虽提到男教师「打算留下来」，但讲的是\n个人意愿，并未把「工作保障」作为留下来所需的条件——属于无中生有。\nC) 张冠李戴\nSocial recognition.谈的是社会层面的认可，原文里「社会文化阻力」出现于P3，而第\n54题的定位段P4谈的是薪资动因——选项把第53题段的话题张冠李戴到第54题。\nD) 以偏概全\nBetter working conditions.说的是更好的工作条件——它是更宽的概念，可能包括薪资\n也可能不包括；但原文明确把动因点到「涨薪」，并未把笼统的「工作条件」列为动\n因，选项以偏概全。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What do the authors of the study recommend to bridge the gender gap in early childhood education?", "options": ["A) Recruiting young men who have a passion for educating young children.", "B) Taking measures to attract prospective male teachers to work in the field.", "C) Persuading prospective fathers to consider a change in their career.", "D) Providing male teachers with more opportunities for advancement."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do the authors of the study recommend to bridge the gender gap in early\nchildhood education?问的是作者明文给出的建议，是显性细节而非主旨。\n【拆句】题干What do the authors of the study recommend to bridge the gender gap in early\nchildhood education?主干是 What do the authors of the study recommend，to\nbridge the gender gap in early childhood education 是不定式短语作状语表目的，结构\n清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】They recommend providing young men opportunities to work with children through training\nand volunteer programs, targeting groups of men who are considering a career change, such\nas fathers.\n他们建议通过培训与志愿项目，给年轻男性提供与儿童一起工作的机会，并把目光投向考虑\n转行的男性群体，例如父亲们。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nRecruiting young men who have a passion for educating young children.给目标人\n群加上 a passion for educating young children 的条件，但原文只说面向 young\nmen 与「考虑转行的男性」，并没限定他们要「有热情」——属于无中生有。\nB) ✓ 正确\nproviding young men opportunities to work with children through training and volunteer\nprograms\n↔ Taking measures to attract prospective male teachers to work in the field\ngroups of men who are considering a career change ↔ prospective male teachers\n预判强调「采取措施吸引潜在男性入行」，Taking measures to attract prospective\nmale teachers to work in the field.恰好把「动作（taking measures）+对象\n（prospective male teachers）+目的（work in the field）」三块都覆盖到位。\nC) 偷换概念\nPersuading prospective fathers to consider a change in their career.里\nprospective fathers 指「准父亲」，但原文such as fathers指的是「已经是父亲的\n人」，且原文是把父亲作为「考虑转行的男性」的一个例子，而非要去「说服准父亲转\n行」——主体身份与动作方向都被偷换。\nD) 与原文相反\nProviding male teachers with more opportunities for advancement.谈的是给已经\n在岗的男教师更多晋升机会，但原文P6的建议是「吸引尚未入行的人入行」，并非「给\n在岗的更多晋升」——动作对象（在岗 vs 未入行）与动作类型（晋升 vs 进入）都与原\n文相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
